ctivity"},{"label":"D","text":"A drug that binds to a receptor and inhibits or opposes cellular activity"},{"label":"E","text":"A drug directed at parasites infecting the patient"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bb348017ff61","stem":"2) Xenobiotics are considered","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endogenous"},{"label":"B","text":"Exogenous"},{"label":"C","text":"Inorganic poisons"},{"label":"D","text":"Toxins"},{"label":"E","text":"Ligands"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-05fd49ac5d36","stem":"3) Which of the following would be a toxin (poison of biological origin)?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pb"},{"label":"B","text":"As"},{"label":"C","text":"Hg"},{"label":"D","text":"Atropine"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-cbfb995ba207","stem":"4) The vast majority of drugs have molecular weights (MW) between 100 and 1,000. Large drugs, such as alteplase (t-PA), must be administered","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Into the compartment where they have their effect"},{"label":"B","text":"Orally so they do not absorb too quickly"},{"label":"C","text":"Rectally to prevent irritation to the stomach lining and vessels"},{"label":"D","text":"Via the intraosseous (IO) route"},{"label":"E","text":"Titrated with buffering agents to prevents cell lysis"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2e083c6ef6fb","stem":"5) Which of the following occurs with drugs that are extremely small, such as Lithium?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Receptor mediated endocytosis"},{"label":"B","text":"Minor drug movement within the body"},{"label":"C","text":"Vasodilation when injected intravenously (IV)"},{"label":"D","text":"Specific receptor binding"},{"label":"E","text":"Nonspecific binding"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8f090d3ef89a","stem":"6) Drugs fit receptors using the lock and key model. Covalent bonds are the ____ and the ____ specific.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Strongest; Most"},{"label":"B","text":"Strongest; Least"},{"label":"C","text":"Weakest; Most"},{"label":"D","text":"Weakest; Least"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-864e5a4d7430","stem":"7) Warfarin (Coumadin) is given as a racemic mixture with the S enantiomer being four times more active than the R enantiomer. If the mixture of Warfarin given is 50% S and 50% R, what is the potency compared with a 100% R enantiomer solution?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4 * R + 1 * S = 1"},{"label":"B","text":"4 * R + 1 * S = 1.5"},{"label":"C","text":"4 * R + 1 * S = 2"},{"label":"D","text":"4 * R + 1 * S = 2.5"},{"label":"E","text":"4 * R + 1 * S = 4"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d23e132f94ea","stem":"8) What determines the degree of movement of a drug between body compartments?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Partition constant"},{"label":"B","text":"Degree of ionization"},{"label":"C","text":"pH"},{"label":"D","text":"Size"},{"label":"E","text":"All of the above"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9a1b78043542","stem":"1) Which of the following is NOT a protein target for drug binding?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Side of action (transport)"},{"label":"B","text":"Enzymes"},{"label":"C","text":"Carrier molecules"},{"label":"D","text":"Receptors"},{"label":"E","text":"Ion channels"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d2ea9cabd726","stem":"2) Which of the following is an example of a drug acting directly through receptors?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Protamine binds stoichiometrically to heparin anticoagulants"},{"label":"B","text":"Adrenergic beta blockers for thyroid hormone-induced tachycardia"},{"label":"C","text":"