opic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypertension"},{"label":"B","text":"Essential tremor"},{"label":"C","text":"Angina"},{"label":"D","text":"Tachycardia"},{"label":"E","text":"Asthma"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-75da8967f37b","stem":"4) Which of the following adverse effects (side-effects) is NOT commonly seen with cholinergic antagonists?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Cholinergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blurred vision"},{"label":"B","text":"Confusion"},{"label":"C","text":"Miosis"},{"label":"D","text":"Constipation"},{"label":"E","text":"Urinary retention"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7887e44439eb","stem":"1) The drug chloramphenicol (Chloromycetin) is risky for which of the following?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neonates"},{"label":"B","text":"Geriatric patients"},{"label":"C","text":"Adult males"},{"label":"D","text":"Obese patients"},{"label":"E","text":"Congestive heart failure patients"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f7da4887f63b","stem":"2) How does the glomerular filtration rate (GFR) change after the age of 40?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increase 1% each year"},{"label":"B","text":"Increases 2% each year"},{"label":"C","text":"Decreases 1% each year"},{"label":"D","text":"Decreases 2% each year"},{"label":"E","text":"Does not depend on age"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d0740b1a146d","stem":"3) A decrease in renal and liver function, as seen in the elderly, would prolong drug half-life, ____ plasma protein binding, and ____ volume of distribution.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increase; Increase"},{"label":"B","text":"Decrease; Decrease"},{"label":"C","text":"Increase; Decrease"},{"label":"D","text":"Decrease; Increase"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-531413d6a35c","stem":"4) When prescribing isoniazid (Rimifon), pharmacogenetics must be considered as >90% of Asians and certain other groups are ____ acetylators, and thus have a ____ blood concentration of a given dose and a decreased risk of toxicity.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Slow; Increased"},{"label":"B","text":"Slow; Decreased"},{"label":"C","text":"Fast; Increased"},{"label":"D","text":"Fast; Decrease"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a1e4e45ac369","stem":"5) Which of the following are the two modifying factors that contribute to why women have higher blood peak concentrations of alcohol than men when consuming equivalent amounts?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lower blood volume & increased hormones"},{"label":"B","text":"Lower fat content & more gastric alcohol dehydrogenase (ADH)"},{"label":"C","text":"Higher fat content & more gastric alcohol dehydrogenase (ADH)"},{"label":"D","text":"Lower fat content & less gastric alcohol dehydrogenase (ADH)"},{"label":"E","text":"Higher fat content & less gastric alcohol dehydrogenase (ADH) 2 – Pharmacokinetic Principles: Drug Movement"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from stem","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-fd039de210ad","stem":"Pharmacokinetics is the effect of the ____ and pharmacodynamics is the effect of the ____.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Pharmacokinetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Drug on a drug; Body on the drug"},{"label":"B","text":"Body on the drug; Drug on a drug"},{"label":"C","text":"Drug on the body; Body on the drug"},{"label":"D","text":"Body on the drug; Drug on the body"},{"label":"E","text":"Drug on a drug; Drug on a drug"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-610cdb73904f","stem":"1) Which of the following is NOT an action of the body on a drug?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Absorption"},{"label":"B","text":"Distribution"},{"label":"C","text":"Metabolism"},{"label":"D","text":"Excretion"},{"label":"E","text":"Side effects"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0ec7a2cc5d56","stem":"If a drug is 80% bound to blood elements or plasma proteins, what part is considered the free form?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"20%"},{"label":"B","text":"40%"},{"label":"C","text":"50%"},{"label":"D","text":"80%"},{"label":"E","text":"100%"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-098ed5ff9f8d","stem":"1) Which of the following describes minimal effective concentration (MEC)?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The minimal drug plasma concentration that can be detected"},{"label":"B","text":"The minimal drug plasma concentration to enter tissues"},{"label":"C","text":"The minimal drug plasma concentration to interact with receptors"},{"label":"D","text":"The minimal drug plasma concentration to produce effect"},{"label":"E","text":"The minimal drug plasma concentration to reach therapeutic levels"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c3066d170e04","stem":"2) If a patient misses three doses of their daily drug, which of the following (in general) is the best solution?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Take a 4x dose at the next dose time"},{"label":"B","text":"Wait 3 more days (week total) then return to normal regimen"},{"label":"C","text":"Do nothing and continue normal regimen"},{"label":"D","text":"Setup an appointment to have the patient evaluated"},{"label":"E","text":"Prescribe a higher dosage pill so missed doses will have less effect"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5097d4f55ef6","stem":"3) Blood levels of a drug correlate to the effectiveness of that drug, such as with pentazocine (Talwin) or phenobarbitol (Luminal).","type":"True / False","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"True"},{"label":"B","text":"False"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8422284685ca","stem":"1) Which of the following drug permeation mechanisms involves polar substances too large to enter cells by other means, such as iron or vitamin B12?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aqueous diffusion"},{"label":"B","text":"Lipid diffusion"},{"label":"C","text":"Carrier molecules"},{"label":"D","text":"Endocytosis and exocytosis"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from stem","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-a48b191caac3","stem":"2) Which of the following drug permeation mechanisms occurs across epithelial tight junctions and is driven by a concentration gradient?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aqueous diffusion"},{"label":"B","text":"Lipid diffusion"},{"label":"C","text":"Carrier molecules"},{"label":"D","text":"Endocytosis and exocytosis"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1acb5da67b51","stem":"3) Which of the following drug permeation mechanisms uses the Henderson- Hasselbalch equation for the ratio of solubility for the weak acid or weak base?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aqueous diffusion"},{"label":"B","text":"Lipid diffusion"},{"label":"C","text":"Carrier molecules"},{"label":"D","text":"Endocytosis and exocytosis"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7312516d0923","stem":"4) Which of the following drug permeation mechanisms is used for peptides, amino acids, glucose, and other large or insoluble molecules?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aqueous diffusion"},{"label":"B","text":"Lipid diffusion"},{"label":"C","text":"Carrier molecules"},{"label":"D","text":"Endocytosis and exocytosis"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-00fa8a802f6e","stem":"5) Which of the following drug permeation mechanisms uses caveolae?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aqueous diffusion"},{"label":"B","text":"Lipid diffusion"},{"label":"C","text":"Carrier molecules"},{"label":"D","text":"Endocytosis and exocytosis"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-34207b1a34ab","stem":"1) Using the Fick Law of Diffusion, how will flux change if membrane thickness is doubled?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"It will double"},{"label":"B","text":"It will quadruple"},{"label":"C","text":"It will halve"},{"label":"D","text":"It will quarter"},{"label":"E","text":"It will not change"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8f3bc0834ad4","stem":"2) Using the Fick Law of Diffusion, how will flux change if the permeability coefficient is quadrupled?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"It will double"},{"label":"B","text":"It will quadruple"},{"label":"C","text":"It will halve"},{"label":"D","text":"It will quarter"},{"label":"E","text":"It will not change"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8ccbfe70998b","stem":"1) Which of the following is the amount of a drug absorbed per the amount administered?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bioavailability"},{"label":"B","text":"Bioequivalence"},{"label":"C","text":"Drug absorption"},{"label":"D","text":"Bioinequivalence"},{"label":"E","text":"Dosage"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option C"]},{"id":"q-5f9f8239d418","stem":"2) Which of the following is NOT needed for drug bioequivalence?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Same active ingredients"},{"label":"B","text":"Same strength or concentration"},{"label":"C","text":"Same dosage form"},{"label":"D","text":"Same route of administration"},{"label":"E","text":"Same side effects"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-13fd462d4063","stem":"3) For intravenous (IV) dosages, what is the bioavailability assumed to be?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0%"},{"label":"B","text":"25%"},{"label":"C","text":"50%"},{"label":"D","text":"75%"},{"label":"E","text":"100%"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4a01cc71c687","stem":"4) Although morphine (Avinza, Oramorph SR, MS Contin) is well-absorbed when administered orally (PO), how much of the drug is metabolized on its first pass through the liver?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"CNS Pharmacology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"CNS Pharmacology","options":[{"label":"A","text":"90%"},{"label":"B","text":"70%"},{"label":"C","text":"50%"},{"label":"D","text":"30%"},{"label":"E","text":"10%"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"CNS Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2192f4d1cc63","stem":"5) For a generic drug to be bioequivalent to an innovator drug (per FDA), it must be measured in ____ of subjects to fall within ____ of the mean of the test population bioavailability.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"50; 50"},{"label":"B","text":"80; 20"},{"label":"C","text":"20; 80"},{"label":"D","text":"95; 5"},{"label":"E","text":"5; 95"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f7cf4af3c65a","stem":"6) Using the FDA bioequivalence rule, how much variation could a generic drug potentially have from an innovator and still be considered equivalent?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"100%"},{"label":"B","text":"20%"},{"label":"C","text":"40%"},{"label":"D","text":"60%"},{"label":"E","text":"80%"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-eec44db17ee8","stem":"1) Which of the following is NOT a pharmacokinetic process?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Pharmacokinetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alteration of the drug by liver enzymes"},{"label":"B","text":"Drug metabolites are removed in the urine"},{"label":"C","text":"Movement of drug from the gut into general circulation"},{"label":"D","text":"The drug causes dilation of coronary vessels"},{"label":"E","text":"The drug is readily deposited in fat tissue"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bb99cf0baee6","stem":"2) Which of the following can produce a therapeutic response? A drug that is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bound to plasma albumin"},{"label":"B","text":"Concentrated in the bile"},{"label":"C","text":"Concentrated in the urine"},{"label":"D","text":"Not absorbed from the GI tract"},{"label":"E","text":"Unbound to plasma proteins"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option B"]},{"id":"q-4aa6f253c8a2","stem":"3) Which of the following most correctly describes steroid hormones with respect to their ability to gain access to intracellular binding sites?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"They cross the cell membrane via aqueous pores"},{"label":"B","text":"They have a high permeability coefficient"},{"label":"C","text":"They are passively transported via membrane carriers"},{"label":"D","text":"They require vesicular transport"},{"label":"E","text":"Their transport requires the hydrolysis of ATP 3 – Pharmacokinetic Principles: pH and Drug Movement"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-658580102a72","stem":"Most drugs are either ____ acids or ____ bases.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Strong; Strong"},{"label":"B","text":"Strong; Weak"},{"label":"C","text":"Weak; Weak"},{"label":"D","text":"Weak; Strong"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8b98ee9dbe8d","stem":"1) Aspirin readily donates a proton in aqueous solutions and pyrimethamine readily accepts a proton in aqueous solution. Thus, aspirin is a(b) ____ and pyrimethamine is a(n) ____.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acid; Base"},{"label":"B","text":"Base; Acid"},{"label":"C","text":"Acid; Acid"},{"label":"D","text":"Base; Base"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ee5f86c5aad2","stem":"2) Given the equilibrium HA <=> A- + H+ (acid) and BH+ <=> B + H+ (base), in an acid environment (low pH) the acid reaction will move to the ____ and the base reaction will move to the ____.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right; Left"},{"label":"B","text":"Right; Right"},{"label":"C","text":"Left; Right"},{"label":"D","text":"Left; Left"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f1c0ec3587fe","stem":"1) What form of a drug is more lipid-soluble, and thus would remain trapped within a compartment where the pH does not favor the lipid-soluble form?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Ph","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Strong acid (A-)"},{"label":"B","text":"Weak acid (A-)"},{"label":"C","text":"Neutral (AH and B)"},{"label":"D","text":"Weak base (BH+)"},{"label":"E","text":"Strong base (BH+)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-37941f6e58f9","stem":"2) The lipid-soluble form of a base is ____ and the lipid-soluble form of an acid is ____.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Base","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Protonated; Protonated"},{"label":"B","text":"Protonated; Unprotonated"},{"label":"C","text":"Unprotonated; Unprotonated"},{"label":"D","text":"Unprotonated; Protonated"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"manualEdits":{"sourceStem":"2) The lipid-soluble form of a base is ____ and the lipid-soluble form of an acid is ____.","sourceOptions":[{"label":"A","text":"Protonated; Protonated"},{"label":"B","text":"Protonated; Unprotonated"},{"label":"C","text":"Unprotonated; Unprotonated"},{"label":"D","text":"Unprotonated; Protonated Version: 16Oct2008 Page 7 of 42 Pharmacology – Part 1 Quiz"}]}},{"id":"q-7469457c7ce9","stem":"1) If the pKa of Aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid) is 3.5 and the pH of the stomach is 2.5, how much Aspirin is in the protonated species in the stomach and is this the amount available for absorption?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"≈ 91%; Yes"},{"label":"B","text":"≈ 91%; No"},{"label":"C","text":"≈ 9%; Yes"},{"label":"D","text":"≈ 9%; No"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-660ab1c3e4bf","stem":"2) What percentage of Aspirin would be ionized in the blood compartment (pH = 7.4) assuming pH is 7.5 and Aspirin pKa is 3.5?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"(10,000 - 1) / 1 = 99.99%"},{"label":"B","text":"(100 - 1) / 1 = 99%"},{"label":"C","text":"None"},{"label":"D","text":"1 / (100 - 1) = 0.9%"},{"label":"E","text":"1 / (10,000 - 1) = 0.009%"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0e3e680928d1","stem":"3) If the pH - pKa = -1, what percentage of weak base is nonionized?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"99"},{"label":"B","text":"90"},{"label":"C","text":"50"},{"label":"D","text":"10"},{"label":"E","text":"1"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a051093a7ba9","stem":"4) If the pH - pka = 2, what percentage of weak acid is nonionized?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"99"},{"label":"B","text":"90"},{"label":"C","text":"50"},{"label":"D","text":"10"},{"label":"E","text":"1"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ec3f31112444","stem":"5) If pH > pKa, the drug is ____ and if pH < pKa, the drug is ____. An unprotonated acid is ____ and a protonated base is ____.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Protonated; Unprotonated; Charged; Charged"},{"label":"B","text":"Protonated; Unprotonated; Neutral; Neutral"},{"label":"C","text":"Unprotonated; Protonated; Charged; Charged"},{"label":"D","text":"Unprotonated; Protonated; Neutral; Charged"},{"label":"E","text":"Unprotonated; Protonated; Charged; Neutral"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9753521e356f","stem":"1) Weak acids are excreted faster in ____ urine and weak bases are excreted faster in ____ urine.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acidic; Alkaline"},{"label":"B","text":"Alkaline; Acidic"},{"label":"C","text":"Acidic; Neutral"},{"label":"D","text":"Neutral; Alkaline"},{"label":"E","text":"Alkaline; Neutral"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"manualEdits":{}},{"id":"q-550d9904c0d6","stem":"2) A patient presents with an overdose of acidic Aspirin. The drug ____ can be given to ____ the pH of the urine and trap the Aspirin, preventing further metabolism.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"NaHCO3; Increase"},{"label":"B","text":"NaHCO3; Decrease"},{"label":"C","text":"NH4Cl; Increase"},{"label":"D","text":"NH4Cl; Decrease"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-5637a1ce5dc0","stem":"3) A patient presents with an overdose of alkaline Codeine. The drug ____ can be given to ____ the pH of the urine and trap the Codeine, preventing further metabolism.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"NaHCO3; Increase"},{"label":"B","text":"NaHCO3; Decrease"},{"label":"C","text":"NH4Cl; Increase"},{"label":"D","text":"NH4Cl; Decrease"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-225e4e40a9d1","stem":"1) The principle of drug manipulation for excretion of a drug out of the renal tubule can be accomplished by","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acidifying the urinary pH"},{"label":"B","text":"Adjusting the urinary pH to protonate weakly acidic drugs"},{"label":"C","text":"Adjusting the urinary pH to unprotonate weakly basic drugs"},{"label":"D","text":"Adjusting the urinary pH to ionize the drug"},{"label":"E","text":"By neutralizing the urinary pH"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ac5806fcb758","stem":"2) Aspirin is a weak organic acid with a pKa of 3.5. What percentage of a given dose will be in the lipid-soluble form at a stomach pH of 1.5?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"About 1%"},{"label":"B","text":"About 10%"},{"label":"C","text":"About 50%"},{"label":"D","text":"About 90%"},{"label":"E","text":"About 99%"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6872f7c9c124","stem":"3) For which of the following drugs is excretion most significantly accelerated by acidification of the urine?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Weak acid with pKa of 5.5"},{"label":"B","text":"Weak acid with pKa of 3.5"},{"label":"C","text":"Weak base with pKa of 7.5"},{"label":"D","text":"Weak base with pKa of 7.1"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6b355e81b7c4","stem":"4) A patient diagnosed with type 2 diabetes is administered an oral dose of 0.1 mg chloropropamide, an insulin secretagogue and weak acid with a pKa of 5.0. What is the amount of this drug that could be absorbed from the stomach at pH 2.0?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Endocrine Pharmacology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Endocrine Pharmacology","options":[{"label":"A","text":"99.9 μg"},{"label":"B","text":"90 μg"},{"label":"C","text":"50 μg"},{"label":"D","text":"0.05 mg"},{"label":"E","text":"0.01 mg 4 – Pharmacokinetic Principles: Absorption"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Endocrine Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-62ae6957ee23","stem":"Bioavailability (F) is the fraction or percentage of administered drug that reaches the systemic circulation via a given route as compared to what route?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oral"},{"label":"B","text":"IV (intravenous)"},{"label":"C","text":"IO (intraosseous)"},{"label":"D","text":"CSF (cerebrospinal fluid)"},{"label":"E","text":"Whatever route attains the target drug concentration in plasma (CT)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-226f879008bf","stem":"What organ is responsible for metabolism in the “first pass effect”?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Metabolism · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Metabolism","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Brain"},{"label":"B","text":"Heart"},{"label":"C","text":"Kidney"},{"label":"D","text":"Liver"},{"label":"E","text":"Spleen"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Metabolism · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"independently_reasoned_provisional_oct20","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option B"]},{"id":"q-dbb2fa6ebc6f","stem":"1) A patient is in the hospital and is stable on digoxin 0.175 mg IV qd (daily). How much digoxin in mg. would you need to give your patient orally, given that the bioavailability for oral digoxin tablets is 0.7?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","options":[{"label":"A","text":"(0.175 * 0.7) / (1.0) = 0.1225 mg"},{"label":"B","text":"(0.175 * 1) / (0.7) = 0.25 mg"},{"label":"C","text":"(0.175 + 0.7) / (1.0) = 0.875 mg"},{"label":"D","text":"(0.175 + 1) / (0.7) = 1.67 mg"},{"label":"E","text":"No change is necessary"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ce60f197fa8f","stem":"2) Given a graph of plasma drug concentration versus time, what part of the graph would be used to calculate bioavailability for a PO (oral) drug administration?","type":"Calculation","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maximum concentration"},{"label":"B","text":"Steady concentration"},{"label":"C","text":"Derivative of the curve (slope)"},{"label":"D","text":"Integral of the curve (area underneath)"},{"label":"E","text":"The curve is not used to calculate bioavailability"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6b14fd267ff6","stem":"1) Which of the following routes of administration has a bioavailability of about 80- 100%, is usually very slow absorbing, and has prolonged duration of action?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IV (intravenous)"},{"label":"B","text":"IM (intramuscular)"},{"label":"C","text":"SQ (subcutaneous)"},{"label":"D","text":"Rectal"},{"label":"E","text":"Transdermal"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d4d1b1cf12fb","stem":"2) Which of the following routers of administration is the most convenient, although may have a bioavailability anywhere from 5-100%?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"PO (oral)"},{"label":"B","text":"IV (intravenous)"},{"label":"C","text":"IM (intramuscular)"},{"label":"D","text":"SQ (subcutaneous)"},{"label":"E","text":"Transdermal"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-24ab29d8b08f","stem":"3) Which of the following enteral administration routes has the largest first-pass effect?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"SL (sublingual)"},{"label":"B","text":"Buccal"},{"label":"C","text":"Rectal"},{"label":"D","text":"Oral"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ff2439daff73","stem":"4) Epithelial cells are connected by ____, which are tough to cross and materials often must pass through the cells. Endothelial cells of blood vessels are connected by ____, which proteins cannot cross but smaller drugs (MW 200-500) can.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Macular gap junctions; Tight junctions"},{"label":"B","text":"Tight junctions; Macular gap junctions"},{"label":"C","text":"Adherens junctions; Tight junctions"},{"label":"D","text":"Tight junctions; Adherens junctions"},{"label":"E","text":"Macular gap junctions; Adherens junctions"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option E"]},{"id":"q-772444e62841","stem":"5) Which of the following administration routes is not often used, is painful, and has a risk of infection and adhesion?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"EPI (epidural)"},{"label":"B","text":"IA (intraarterial)"},{"label":"C","text":"IP (intraperitoneal)"},{"label":"D","text":"IV (intravenous)"},{"label":"E","text":"IO (intraosseous)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAudi