type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Liver"},{"label":"B","text":"Brain"},{"label":"C","text":"Fat"},{"label":"D","text":"Muscle"},{"label":"E","text":"Kidney"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-46c5aaeefc1c","stem":"2) Which of the following is the least important for passage through capillary walls but the most important for passage through the cell wall?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Molecular size"},{"label":"B","text":"Lipid solubility"},{"label":"C","text":"Diffusion constant"},{"label":"D","text":"pH"},{"label":"E","text":"pKa"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5054ff4e1e79","stem":"3) Which of the following is the most important for movement through capillary walls?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Molecular size"},{"label":"B","text":"Lipid solubility"},{"label":"C","text":"Diffusion constant"},{"label":"D","text":"pH"},{"label":"E","text":"pKa"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3496c7b70f34","stem":"4) Which of the following locations would most trap a lipid soluble drug?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blood"},{"label":"B","text":"Intestines"},{"label":"C","text":"Brain"},{"label":"D","text":"Stomach"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-e027d488c57a","stem":"5) What type of drugs can cross the blood-brain barrier (BBB)?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Large and lipid-soluble"},{"label":"B","text":"Large and lipid-insoluble"},{"label":"C","text":"Small and lipid-soluble"},{"label":"D","text":"Small and lipid-insoluble"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-79b2b3877432","stem":"1) Acidic drugs, such as phenytoin, bind primarily to which of the following plasma proteins?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"α1-fetoprotein (AFP)"},{"label":"B","text":"GC Globulin"},{"label":"C","text":"Albumin"},{"label":"D","text":"α1-acid glycoprotein (AAG)"},{"label":"E","text":"Transcortin"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c3d436c1d654","stem":"2) Basic drugs, such as lidocaine, bind primarily to which of the following plasma proteins?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"α1-fetoprotein (AFP)"},{"label":"B","text":"Gc-Globulin (GcG)"},{"label":"C","text":"Albumin"},{"label":"D","text":"α1-acid glycoprotein (AAG)"},{"label":"E","text":"Transcortin"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-77e43f4e98ef","stem":"1) A decrease in drug-protein binding will lead to which of the following?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decrease in the unbound drug concentration"},{"label":"B","text":"Increase in free drug"},{"label":"C","text":"Increase in rate of drug elimination"},{"label":"D","text":"Decrease in volume of distribution"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-878c71a3e370","stem":"2) A patient presents with acute-onset cirrhosis of the liver. They are found to have hypoalbuminemia. In severe cirrhosis it is expected that AAG will be decreased, but the patient presents with increased AAG due to the inflammatory response. Which of the following is the most likely?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology","topicOriginal":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology","subtopic":"Cirrhosis","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased acidic drug binding and increased basic drug binding"},{"label":"B","text":"Increased acidic drug binding and decreased basic drug binding"},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased acidic drug binding and increased basic drug binding"},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased acidic drug binding and decreased basic drug binding"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Internal Medicine","displayTopic":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d758c5c1a74b","stem":"3) Which of the following is NOT a site of loss (where drug is not used)?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fat"},{"label":"B",