 most likely for finding a free, unaltered drug?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Urine"},{"label":"B","text":"Feces"},{"label":"C","text":"Breast milk"},{"label":"D","text":"Fat"},{"label":"E","text":"Sweat"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d1e3d4e858cb","stem":"2) Most drugs are active in their ____ form and inactive in their ____ form.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Non-polar; Polar"},{"label":"B","text":"Polar; Non-polar"},{"label":"C","text":"Water-soluble; Lipid-soluble"},{"label":"D","text":"Lipid-insoluble; Water-insoluble"},{"label":"E","text":"Neutral; Neutral"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8c2c1f1c95a2","stem":"1) Drug biotransformation phase I makes drugs ____ polar for metabolism and phase II makes drugs ____ polar for excretion.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"More; More"},{"label":"B","text":"More; Less"},{"label":"C","text":"Less; More"},{"label":"D","text":"Less; Less"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d71ad4314f54","stem":"2) Which of the following is NOT a phase II substrate?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glucuronic acid"},{"label":"B","text":"Sulfuric acid"},{"label":"C","text":"Acetic acid"},{"label":"D","text":"Amino acids"},{"label":"E","text":"Alcohol"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-474e5fc38606","stem":"Which of the following reactions is phase II and NOT phase I?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxidations"},{"label":"B","text":"Reductions"},{"label":"C","text":"Conjugations"},{"label":"D","text":"Deaminations"},{"label":"E","text":"Hydrolyses"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2047319d878b","stem":"Which of the following metabolically active tissues is the principle organ for drug metabolism?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Metabolism · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Metabolism","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Skin"},{"label":"B","text":"Kidneys"},{"label":"C","text":"Lungs"},{"label":"D","text":"Liver"},{"label":"E","text":"GI Tract"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Metabolism · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"manualEdits":{}},{"id":"q-6e663a4cf2ad","stem":"1) Damage at which of the following locations would most affect the goals of phase II biotransformation?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Skin"},{"label":"B","text":"Kidneys"},{"label":"C","text":"Lungs"},{"label":"D","text":"Liver"},{"label":"E","text":"GI Tract Match the biotransformation reaction with the drug:"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option B"]},{"id":"q-9f756620420e","stem":"1) What is the goal of the P450 system (microsomes pinched off from endoplasmic reticulum)?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metabolism of substances"},{"label":"B","text":"Detoxification of substances"},{"label":"C","text":"Increasing pH of compartments containing substances"},{"label":"D","text":"Decreasing pH of compartments containing substances"},{"label":"E","text":"A & B"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f72d87650afc","stem":"2) Regarding the microsomal drug metabolizing system, a patient with late stage alcoholism and liver damage would have more ETOH available due to which of the following concepts?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased induction"},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased induction"},{"label":"C","text":"Increased inhibition"},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased inhibition"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d6ef3d9c0db2","stem":"3) Regarding the microsomal drug metabolizing system, a patient who is a chronic user of barbiturates would need more drug to produce the same effects due to which of the following concepts?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased induction"},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased induction"},{"label":"C","text":"Increased inhibition"},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased inhibition"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-19b4af211883","stem":"4) Which of the following are the drugs that induce CYP 1A2 and the drugs that have their metabolism induced by 1A2?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbamazepine & phenobarbitol; Theophyline & warfarin"},{"label":"B","text":"Phenobarbitol & phenytoin ; Phenytoin & warfarin"},{"label":"C","text":"Carbamazepine & phenytoin; Warfarin"},{"label":"D","text":"Carbamazepine; Cyclosporine"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a8bf76e9ce09","stem":"5) Which of the following are the drugs that inhibit CYP 1A2 and the drugs that have their metabolism inhibited by 1A2?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"SSRIs; Phenytoin & warfarin"},{"label":"B","text":"Amiodarone & cimetidine; Phenytoin & warfarin"},{"label":"C","text":"Cimetidine, erythromycin, & grapefruit juice; Theophyline & warfarin"},{"label":"D","text":"Cimetidine & erythromycin; Cyclosporine"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags