d set of drugs. biochemistry. The chapter-based approach facilitates use of Ninth, after you have worked your way through most or this book in conjunction with course notes or a larger text. all of the chapters and have a good grasp of the Key Drugs, We recommend several strategies to make reviewing more you should take the comprehensive examinations, each of effective. 100 questions, presented in Appendices II and III. These First, each chapter has a short discussion of the major examinations are followed by a list of answers each with a concepts that underlie its basic principles or the specific drug short explanation or rationale underlying the correct choice group, accompanied by explanatory figures and tables. The and the numbers of the chapters in which more information figures are in full color and many are new to this edition. can be found if needed. We recommend that you take an Students are advised to read the text thoroughly before they entire examination or a block of questions as if it were a real attempt to answer the study questions at the end of each examination: commit to answers for the whole set before you chapter. If a concept is found to be difficult or confusing, the check the answers. As you work through the answers, make student is advised to consult a regular textbook such as Basic sure that you understand why each distractor is either correct & Clinical Pharmacology, 12th edition. or incorrect. If you need to, return to the relevant chapters(s) Second, each drug-oriented chapter opens with an to review the text that covers key concepts and facts that form “Overview” that organizes the group of drugs visually in the basis for the question. diagrammatic form. We recommend that students practice Tenth, you can use the strategies in Appendix IV for reproducing the overview diagram from memory. improving your test performance. General advice for study- Third, a list of High Yield Terms to Learn and their defi- ing and approaching examinations includes strategies for nitions is near the front of most chapters. Make sure that you several types of questions that follow specific formats. are able to define those terms. We recommend that this book be used with a regular text. Fourth, many chapters include a “Skill Keeper” question Basic & Clinical Pharmacology, 12th edition (McGraw-Hill, that prompts the student to review previous material and to 2012), follows the chapter sequence used here. However, see links between related topics. We suggest that students this review book is designed to complement any standard try to answer Skill Keeper questions on their own before medical pharmacology text. The student who completes and checking the answers that are provided at the end of the understands Pharmacology: Examination & Board Review chapter. will greatly improve his or her performance and will have an Fifth, each of the sixty-one chapters contains upto ten excellent command of pharmacology. sample questions followed by a set of answers with explana- Because it was developed in parallel with the textbook tions. For most effective learning, you should take each set of Basic & Clinical Pharmacology, this review book represents sample questions as if it were a real examination. After you the authors’ interpretations of chapters written by contribu- have answered every question, work through the answers. tors to that text. We are grateful to those contributors, to our When you are analyzing the answers, make sure that you other faculty colleagues, and to our students, who have taught understand why each choice is either correct or incorrect. us most of what we know about teaching. v vi PREFACE We welcome the participation of Marieke Kruidering- Lebowitz, and Michael Weitz. The authors also thank Alice Hall, PhD, a recipient of the Distinguished Teaching Award Camp for her excellent proofreading contributions to this and of the University of California, San Francisco, as a co-author earlier editions. of this 10th edition of Pharmacology: Examination & Board Review. Anthony J. Trevor, PhD We very much appreciate the invaluable contributions to Bertram G. Katzung, MD, PhD this text afforded by the editorial team of Karen Edmonson, Marieke Kruidering-Hall, PhD Rachel D’Annucci Henriquez, Harleen Chopra, Harriet Susan B. Masters, PhD PART I BASIC PRINCIPLES C H A P T E R Introduction 1 Pharmacology is the body of knowledge concerned with the systems. Pharmacokinetics describes the effects of the body action of chemicals on biologic systems. Medical pharmacology on drugs, eg, absorption, excretion, etc. Pharmacodynamics is the area of pharmacology concerned with the use of chemi- denotes the actions of the drug on the body, such as mechanism cals in the prevention, diagnosis, and treatment of disease, of action and therapeutic and toxic effects. This chapter intro- especially in humans. Toxicology is the area of pharmacology duces the basic principles of pharmacokinetics and pharmaco- concerned with the undesirable effects of chemicals on biologic dynamics that will be applied in subsequent chapters. Introduction Definitions Pharmacodynamics Pharmacokinetics Nature of drugs Permeability Receptors, Henderson- receptor sites Hasselbalch Inert binding sites Distribution Elimination THE NATURE OF DRUGS are partially or completely synthetic. Many biologically important endogenous molecules and exogenous drugs are optically active; Drugs in common use include inorganic ions, nonpeptide organic that is, they contain one or more asymmetric centers and can exist molecules, small peptides and proteins, nucleic acids, lipids, and as enantiomers. The enantiomers of optically active drugs usually carbohydrates. Some are found in plants or animals, but many differ, sometimes more than 1000-fold, in their affinity for their 1 2 PART I Basic Principles biologic receptor sites. Furthermore, such enantiomers may be their actions, whereas drugs much larger than MW 1000 are often metabolized at different rates in the body, with important clinical poorly absorbed and poorly distributed in the body. consequences.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Pharmacokinetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"B","text":"Drug-Receptor Bonds"},{"label":"A","text":"Size and Molecular Weight Drugs bind to receptors with a variety of chemical bonds. These Drugs vary in size from molecular weight (MW) 7 (lithium) to include very strong covalent bonds (which usually result in over MW 50,000 (thrombolytic enzymes, other proteins). Most irreversible action), somewhat weaker electrostatic bonds (eg, drugs, however, have molecular weights between 100 and 1000. between a cation and an anion), and much weaker interactions Drugs smaller than MW 100 are rarely sufficiently selective in (eg, hydrogen, van der Waals, and hydrophobic bonds). PHARMACODYNAMIC PRINCIPLES"},{"label":"A","text":"Receptors and Receptor Sites uric acid). These drugs do not act on endogenous receptors but on endogenous substrate molecules. Drug actions are mediated through the effects of drug molecules on drug receptors in the body. Most receptors are large regulatory molecules that influence important biochemical processes (eg,"},{"label":"B","text":"Inert Binding Sitesenzymes involved in glucose metabolism) or physiologic processes (eg, neurotransmitter receptors, neurotransmitter reuptake trans- Because most drug molecules are much smaller than their receptor porters, and ion transporters). molecules (discussed in the text that follows), specific regions of If drug-receptor binding results in activation of the receptor, receptor molecules often can be identified that provide the local the drug is termed an agonist; if inhibition results, the drug is areas for drug binding. Such areas are termed receptor sites. In considered an antagonist. Some drugs mimic agonist molecules addition, drugs bind to other nonregulatory molecules in the by inhibiting metabolic enzymes, eg, acetylcholinesterase inhibi- body without producing a discernible effect. Such binding sites tors. As suggested in Figure 1–1, a receptor molecule may have are termed inert binding sites. In some compartments of the several binding sites. Quantitation of the effects of drug-receptor body (eg, the plasma), inert binding sites play an important role binding as a function of dose yields dose-response curves that in buffering the concentration of a drug because bound drug does provide information about the nature of the drug-receptor inter- not contribute directly to the concentration gradient that drives action. Dose-response phenomena are discussed in more detail in diffusion. Albumin and orosomucoid (α1-acid glycoprotein) Chapter 2. A few drugs are enzymes themselves (eg, thrombolytic are two important plasma proteins with significant drug-binding enzymes that dissolve blood clots; pegloticase, which metabolizes capacity. High-Yield Terms to Learn Drugs Substances that act on biologic systems at the chemical (molecular) level and alter their functions Drug receptors The molecular components of the body with which drugs interact to bring about their effects Distribution phase The phase of drug movement from the site of administration into the tissues Elimination phase The phase of drug inactivation or removal from the body by metabolism or excretion Endocytosis, exocytosis Endocytosis: Absorption of material across a cell membrane by enclosing it in cell membrane material and pulling it into the cell, where it can be released. Exocytosis: Expulsion of material from vesicles in the cell into the extracellular space Permeation Movement of a molecule (eg, drug) through the biologic medium Pharmacodynamics The actions of a drug on the body, including receptor interactions, dose-response phenomena, and mechanisms of therapeutic and toxic actions Pharmacokinetics The actions of the body on the drug, including absorption, distribution, metabolism, and elimination. Elimination of a drug may be achieved by metabolism or by excretion. Biodisposition is a term sometimes used to describe the processes of metabolism and excretion Transporter A specialized molecule, usually a protein, that carries a drug, transmitter, or other molecule across a membrane in which it is not permeable, eg, Na+/K+ ATPase, serotonin reuptake transporter, etc CHAPTER 1 Introduction 3 Drug Receptor Effects A Agonist + A+C A alone – Response A+B B A+D Log Dose Competitive inhibitor C Allosteric activator D Allosteric inhibitor FIGURE 1–1 Potential mechanisms of drug interaction with a receptor. Possible effects resulting from these interactions are diagrammed in the dose-response curves at the right. The traditional agonist (drug A)-receptor binding process results in the dose-response curve denoted “A alone.” B is a pharmacologic antagonist drug that competes with the agonist for binding to the receptor site. The dose-response curve produced by increasing doses of A in the presence of a fixed concentration of B is indicated by the curve “A+B.” Drugs C and D act at different sites on the receptor molecule; they are allosteric activators or inhibitors. Note that allosteric inhibitors do not compete with the agonist drug for binding to the receptor, and they may bind reversibly or irreversibly. (Reproduced, with permission, from Katzung BG, editor: Basic & Clinical Pharmacology, 12th ed. McGraw-Hill, 2012: Fig. 1–3.) PHARMACOKINETIC PRINCIPLES To produce useful therapeutic effects, most drugs must be A. Permeation absorbed, distributed, and eliminated. Pharmacokinetic principles Permeation is the movement of drug molecules into and withinmake rational dosing possible by quantifying these processes. the biologic environment. It involves several processes, the most important of which are discussed next. THE MOVEMENT OF DRUGS IN THE BODY"}],"priority":2,"status":"quarantine","flags":["probable_document_or