e concentra- for most drugs. Therefore, knowledge of the time course of con- tions of these drugs in plasma decrease in a linear fashion over centration in plasma is important in predicting the intensity and time. This is typical of ethanol (over most of its plasma concentra- duration of effect for most drugs. Note: Drug elimination is not tion range) and of phenytoin and aspirin at high therapeutic or the same as drug excretion: A drug may be eliminated by metabo- toxic concentrations. lism long before the modified molecules are excreted from the body. For most drugs and their metabolites, excretion is primarily by way of the kidney. Anesthetic gases, a major exception, are excreted primarily by the lungs. For drugs with active metabolites PHARMACOKINETIC MODELS (eg, diazepam), elimination of the parent molecule by metabolism is not synonymous with termination of action. For drugs that are A. Multicompartment Distribution not metabolized, excretion is the mode of elimination. A small After absorption into the circulation, many drugs undergo annumber of drugs combine irreversibly with their receptors, so early distribution phase followed by a slower elimination phase.that disappearance from the bloodstream is not equivalent to cessa- Mathematically, this behavior can be simulated by means of ation of drug action: These drugs may have a very prolonged action. “two-compartment model” as shown in Figure 1–4. The twoFor example, phenoxybenzamine, an irreversible inhibitor of compartments consist of the blood and the extravascular tis-α adrenoceptors, is eliminated from the bloodstream in less than sues. (Note that each phase is associated with a characteristic1 h after administration. The drug’s action, however, lasts for 48 h, half-life: t1/2α for the first phase, t1/2β for the second phase.the time required for turnover of the receptors. Note also that when concentration is plotted on a logarithmic axis, the elimination phase for a first-order drug is a straight"},{"label":"A","text":"First-Order Elimination line.) The term first-order elimination implies that the rate of elimination is proportional to the concentration (ie, the higher the concentra- tion, the greater the amount of drug eliminated per unit time). The"},{"label":"B","text":"Other Distribution Models result is that the drug’s concentration in plasma decreases exponen- tially with time (Figure 1–3, left). Drugs with first-order elimina- A few drugs behave as if they were distributed to only 1 compart- tion have a characteristic half-life of elimination that is constant ment (eg, if they are restricted to the vascular compartment). regardless of the amount of drug in the body. The concentration of Others have more complex distributions that require more than such a drug in the blood will decrease by 50% for every half-life. 2 compartments for construction of accurate mathematical Most drugs in clinical use demonstrate first-order kinetics. models. First-order elimination Zero-order elimination"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-d77be9abc414","stem":"A 12-year-old child has bacterial pharyngitis and is to receive 10. A new drug was administered intravenously, and its plasma an oral antibiotic. Ampicillin is a weak organic acid with a levels were measured for several hours. A graph was prepared pKa of 2.5. What percentage of a given dose will be in the as shown below, with the plasma levels plotted on a logarith- lipid-soluble form in the duodenum at a pH of 4.5? mic ordinate and time on a linear abscissa. It was concluded (A) About 1% that the drug has first-order kinetics. From this graph, what (B) About 10% is the best estimate of the half-life? (C) About 50% (D) About 90% (E) About 99%","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["possible_multiple_questions_in_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0256b6da4422","stem":"Ampicillin is eliminated by first-order kinetics. Which of the 32 following statements best describes the process by which the plasma concentration of this drug declines?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e8409a892826","stem":"Which of the following statements is most correct regarding Time (h) the termination of drug action?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":fa