fect opposite to that produced by the drug it a very safe drug might be expected to have a very large toxic dose antagonizes. Thus, it differs from a pharmacologic antagonist, and a much smaller effective dose. For example, in Figure 2–2, which interacts with the same receptor as the drug it inhibits. the ED50 is approximately 3 mg, and the LD50 is approximately Familiar examples of physiologic antagonists are the antagonism 150 mg. The therapeutic index is therefore approximately 150/3, of the bronchoconstrictor action of histamine by epinephrine’s or 50, in mice. Obviously, a full range of toxic doses cannot be bronchodilator action and glucagon’s antagonism of the cardiac ethically studied in humans. Furthermore, factors such as the effects of propranolol. varying slopes of dose-response curves make this estimate a poor safety index even in animals."},{"label":"C","text":"Chemical Antagonists The therapeutic window, a more clinically useful index of safety, describes the dosage range between the minimum effec- A chemical antagonist interacts directly with the drug being tive therapeutic concentration or dose, and the minimum toxic antagonized to remove it or to prevent it from binding to its concentration or dose. For example, if the average minimum target. A chemical antagonist does not depend on interaction therapeutic plasma concentration of theophylline is 8 mg/L and with the agonist’s receptor (although such interaction may occur). toxic effects are observed at 18 mg/L, the therapeutic window is Common examples of chemical antagonists are dimercaprol, a 8–18 mg/L. Both the therapeutic index and the therapeutic win- chelator of lead and some other toxic metals, and pralidoxime, dow depend on the specific toxic effect used in the determination. which combines avidly with the phosphorus in organophosphate cholinesterase inhibitors. SIGNALING MECHANISMS Once an agonist drug has bound to its receptor, some effector mechanism is activated. The receptor-effector system may be an SKILL KEEPER: ALLOSTERIC ANTAGONISTS enzyme in the intracellular space (eg, cyclooxygenase, a target of (SEE CHAPTER 1) nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs) or in the membrane or extracellular space (eg, acetylcholinesterase). Neurotransmitter Describe the difference between a pharmacologic antagonist reuptake transporters (eg, the norepinephrine transporter, NET, and an allosteric inhibitor. How could you differentiate these and the dopamine transporter, DAT, are receptors for many two experimentally? drugs, eg, antidepressants and cocaine. Most antiarrhythmic 18 PART I Basic Principles Steroid Tyrosine JAK-STAT Ion Channel GPCR kinase Drug Outside cell Membrane G Inside cell JAK A B Y Y~P X Y STAT FIGURE 2–6 Signaling mechanisms for drug effects. Five major signaling mechanisms are recognized: (1) transmembrane diffusion of the drug to bind to an intracellular receptor; (2) transmembrane enzyme receptors, whose outer domain provides the receptor function and inner domain provides the effector mechanism converting A to B; (3) transmembrane receptors that, after activation by an appropriate ligand, activate separate cytoplasmic tyrosine kinase molecules (JAKs), which phosphorylate STAT molecules that regulate transcription (Y, tyrosine; P, phosphate); (4) transmembrane channels that are gated open or closed by the binding of a drug to the receptor site; and (5) G protein-coupled receptors, which use a coupling protein to activate a separate effector molecule. (Modified and reproduced, with permission, from Katzung BG, editor: Basic & Clinical Pharmacology, 12th ed. McGraw-Hill, 2012: Fig. 2–5.) drugs target voltage-activated ion channels in the membrane for RECEPTOR REGULATION sodium, potassium, or calcium. For the largest group of drug- receptor interactions, the drug is present in the extracellular space, Receptors are dynamically regulated in number, location, and whereas the effector mechanism resides inside the cell and modi- sensitivity. Changes can occur over short times (minutes) and fies some intracellular process. These classic drug-receptor inter- longer periods (days). actions involve signaling across the membrane. Five major types Frequent or continuous exposure to agonists often results in short- of transmembrane-signaling mechanisms for receptor-effector term diminution of the receptor response, sometimes called tachy- systems have been defined (Figure 2–6, Table 2–1). phylaxis. Several mechanisms are responsible for this phenomenon. TABLE 2–1 Types of transmembrane signaling receptors. Receptor Type Description Steroid-like Steroids, vitamin D, nitric oxide, and a few other highly membrane-permeable agents cross the membrane and activate intracellular receptors. The effector molecule may be part of the receptor or separate Membrane-spanning Insulin, epidermal growth factor, and similar agents bind to the extracellular domain of molecules that incorporate receptor-effector enzymes tyrosine kinase enzyme activity in their intracellular domains. Most of these receptors dimerize upon activation Membrane receptors that Many cytokines activate receptor molecules that bind intracellular tyrosine kinase enzymes (Janus kinases, JAKs) that bind intracellular tyrosine activate transcription regulators (signal transducers and activators of transcription, STATs) that migrate to the nucleus kinase enzymes to bring about the final effect Ligand-activated or Certain Na+/K+ channels are activated by drugs: acetylcholine activates nicotinic Na+/K+ channels, serotonin activates modulated membrane 5-HT3 Na+/K+ channels. Benzodiazepines and several other sedative hypnotics allosterically modulate GABA-activated ion channels Cl– channels"},{"label":"G","text":"protein-coupled receptors GPCRs consist of 7 transmembrane (7-TM) domains and when activated by extracellular ligands, bind trimeric (GPCRs) G proteins and cause the release of activated Gα and Gβγ units. These activated units, in turn, modulate cytoplasmic effectors. The effectors commonly synthesize or release second messengers such as cAMP, IP3, and DAG. GPCRs are the most common type of receptors in the body cAMP, cyclic adenosine monophosphate; IP3, inositol trisphosphate; DAG, diacylglycerol. CHAPTER 2 Pharmacodynamics 19 First, intracellular molecules may block access of a G protein to the 3. Prior to clinical trials in patients with heart failure, an animal activated receptor molecule. For example, the molecule β-arrestin has study was carried out to compare two new positive inotropic been shown to bind to an intracellular loop of the β adrenoceptor drugs (A and B) to a current standard agent (C). The results of cardiac output measurements are shown in the graphwhen the receptor is continuously activated. Beta-arrestin prevents below. access of the Gs-coupling protein and thus desensitizes the tissue to further β-agonist activation within minutes. Removal of the β agonist results in removal of β-arrestin and restoration of the full response B after a few minutes or hours. Second, agonist-bound receptors may be internalized by Cendocytosis, removing them from further exposure to extracel- output lular molecules. The internalized receptor molecule may then be Aeither reinserted into the membrane (eg, morphine receptors) or cardiac degraded (eg, β adrenoceptors, epidermal growth factor receptors). in In some cases, the internalization-reinsertion process may actu- ally be necessary for normal functioning of the receptor-effector Increase system. Third, continuous activation of the receptor-effector system may lead to depletion of some essential substrate required for downstream effects. For example, depletion of thiol cofactors may Log dose be responsible for tolerance to nitroglycerin. In some cases, reple- tion of the missing substrate (eg, by administration of glutathione) Which of the following statements is correct?can reverse the tolerance. (A) Drug A is most effective Long-term reductions in receptor number (downregulation) (B) Drug B is least potent may occur in response to continuous exposure to agonists. The (C) Drug C is most potent opposite change (upregulation) occurs when receptor activation (D) Drug B is more potent than drug C and more effective is blocked for prolonged periods (usually several days) by pharma- than drug A cologic antagonists or by denervation. (E) Drug A is more potent than drug B and more effective than drug C"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-06ee969bae26","stem":"A 55-year-old woman with hypertension is to be treated with highly effective β stimulants, however, pindolol caused a a vasodilator drug. Drugs X and Y have the same mechanism dose-dependent, reversible decrease in heart rate. Which of of action. Drug X in a dose of 5 mg produces the same the following expressions best describes pindolol? decrease in blood pressure as 500 mg of drug Y. Which of the (A) A chemical antagonist following statements best describes these results? (B) An irreversible antagonist (A) Drug Y is less efficacious than drug X (C) A partial agonist (B) Drug X is about 100 times more potent than drug Y (D) A physiologic antagonist (C) Toxicity of drug X is less than that of drug Y (E) A spare receptor agonist (D) Drug X has a wider therapeutic window than drug Y (E) Drug X will have a shorter duration of action than drug 5. Beta adrenoceptors in the heart regulate cardiac rate and Y because less of drug X is present over the time course contractile strength. Several studies have indicated that in of drug action humans and experimental animals, about 90% of β adreno- ceptors in the heart are spare receptors. Which of the follow","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-47d3886baa19","stem":"Graded and quantal dose-response curves are being used for ing statements about spare receptors is most correct? evaluation of a new antiasthmatic drug in the animal labora- (A) Spare receptors, in the absence of drug, are sequestered in tory and in clinical trials. Which of the following statements the cytoplasm best describes quantal dose-response curves? (B) Spare receptors may be detected by finding that the drug- (A) More precisely quantitated than graded dose-response receptor interaction lasts longer than the intracellular curves effect (B) Obtainable from the study of intact subjects but not (C) Spare receptors influence the maximal efficacy of the from isolated tissue preparations drug-receptor system (C) Used to determine the maximal efficacy of the drug (D) Spare receptors activate the effector machinery of the cell (D) Used to determine the statistical variation (standard without the need for a drug deviation) of the maximal response to the drug (E) Spare receptors may be detected by the finding that the (E) Used to determine the variation in sensitivity of subjects EC50 is smaller than the Kd for the agonist to the drug 20 PART I Basic Principles","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-c80a59edc01c","stem":"Two cholesterol-lowering drugs, X and Y, were studied in 9. Which of the curves in the graph describes the percentage a large group of patients, and the percentages of the group effect observed when a large dose of full agonist is present showing a specific therapeutic effect (35% reduction in low- throughout the experiment and the concentration of a partial density lipoprotein [LDL] cholesterol) were determined. The agonist is increased from low to very high levels? results are shown in the following table. (A) Curve 1 Percent Percent (B) Curve 2 Drug Dose Responding to Responding to (C) Curve 3 (mg) Drug X Drug Y (D) Curve 4 (E) Curve 5 5 1 10 10 5 20 10. Which of the curves in the graph describes the percentage of 20 10 50 binding of the partial agonist whose effect is shown by Curve 4 if 50 50 70 the system has many spare receptors? 100 70 90 (A) Curve 1 200 90 100 (B) Curve 2 (C) Curve 3 Which of the following statements about these results is correct? (D) Curve 4 (A) Drug X is safer than drug Y (E) Curve 5 (B) Drug Y is more effective than drug X (C) The 2 drugs act on the same receptors (D) Drug X is less potent than drug Y (E) The therapeutic index of drug Y is 10 ANSWERS","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publi