y is calculated from AUC(route)/AUC(IV). EXTRACTION Q Liver Q Removal of a drug by an organ can be specified as the extraction Portal Ci Co Systemic circulation circulation ratio, that is, the fraction or percentage of the drug removed from the perfusing blood during its passage through the organ CLliver Intravenous (Figure 3–5). After steady-state concentration in plasma has been Oral dose dose achieved, the extraction ratio is one measure of the elimination of the drug by that organ. Gut Remainder Drugs that have a high hepatic extraction ratio have a large first-pass effect; the bioavailability of these drugs after oral admin- of the body istration is low. CLother CLrenal SKILL KEEPER 2: FIRST-PASS EFFECT FIGURE 3–5 The principles of organ extraction and first-pass (SEE CHAPTER 1) effect are illustrated. Part of the administered oral dose (blue) is lost to metabolism in the gut and the liver before it enters the systemic circulation: This is the first-pass effect. The extraction of drug from The oral route of administration is the most likely to have a the circulation by the liver is equal to blood flow times the difference large first-pass effect and therefore low bioavailability. What between entering and leaving drug concentration, ie, Q × (Ci – Co). CL, tissues contribute to this effect? The Skill Keeper Answer clearance. (Modified and reproduced, with permission, from Katzung appears at the end of the chapter. BG, editor: Basic & Clinical Pharmacology, 8th ed. McGraw-Hill, 2001.) CHAPTER 3 Pharmacokinetics 27 DOSAGE REGIMENS Minimum toxic A dosage regimen is a plan for drug administration over a period concentration Therapeutic 20 window of time. An optimal dosage regimen results in the achievement of therapeutic levels of the drug in the blood without exceeding the (mg/L)minimum toxic concentration. To maintain the plasma concen- Cp 10tration within a specified range over long periods of therapy, a schedule of maintenance doses is used. If it is necessary to achieve Minimum effective the target plasma level rapidly, a loading dose is used to “load” the concentration 0 Vd with the drug. Ideally, the dosing plan is based on knowledge of both the minimum therapeutic and minimum toxic concentra- 0 5 10 15 tions for the drug, as well as its clearance and Vd. Time (h) part of the dose that is eliminated by the kidney. For example, if plasma concentrations of 4 mg/L? 20 mL/min rapidly, how much should be given?","type":"Calculation","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Pharmacokinetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maintenance Dosage FIGURE 3–6 The therapeutic window for theophylline in a typical patient. The minimum effective concentration in this patient Because the maintenance rate of drug administration is equal to was found to be 8 mg/L; the minimum toxic concentration was found the rate of elimination at steady state (this is the definition of to be 16 mg/L. The therapeutic window is indicated by the blue area. steady state), the maintenance dosage is a function of clearance To maintain the plasma concentration (Cp) within the window, this (from Equation 2). drug must be given at least once every half-life (7.5 h in this patient) because the minimum effective concentration is half the minimum CL × Desired plasma concentratiton toxic concentration and Cp will decay by 50% in 1 half-life. (Note: This Dosing rate = (4) concept applies to drugs given in the ordinary, prompt-release form. Bioavailability Slow-release formulations can often be given at longer intervals.) Note that Vd is not involved in the calculation of maintenance dosing rate. The dosing rate computed for maintenance dosage is the average dose per unit time. When performing such calcula- tions, make certain that the units are in agreement throughout. THERAPEUTIC WINDOW For example, if clearance is given in mL/min, the resulting dosing rate is a per minute rate. Because convenience of administration is The therapeutic window is the safe range between the minimum desirable for chronic therapy, doses should be given orally if pos- therapeutic concentration and the minimum toxic concentration sible and only once or a few times per day. The size of the daily of a drug. The concept is used to determine the acceptable range dose (dose per minute × 60 min/h × 24 h/d) is a simple extension of plasma levels when designing a dosing regimen. Thus, the of the preceding information. The number of doses to be given minimum effective concentration usually determines the desired per day is usually determined by the half-life of the drug and the trough levels of a drug given intermittently, whereas the mini- difference between the minimum therapeutic and toxic concentra- mum toxic concentration determines the permissible peak plasma tions (see Therapeutic Window, next). concentration. For example, the drug theophylline has a therapeu- If it is important to maintain a concentration above the mini- tic concentration range of 8–20 mg/L but may be toxic at con- mum therapeutic level at all times, either a larger dose is given at centrations of more than 15 mg/L. The therapeutic window for long intervals or smaller doses at more frequent intervals. If the a patient might thus be 8–16 mg/L (Figure 3–6). Unfortunately, difference between the toxic and therapeutic concentrations is for some drugs the therapeutic and toxic concentrations vary so small, then smaller and more frequent doses must be administered greatly among patients that it is impossible to predict the thera- to prevent toxicity. peutic window in a given patient. Such drugs must be titrated individually in each patient."},{"label":"B","text":"Loading Dosage If the therapeutic concentration must be achieved rapidly and ADJUSTMENT OF DOSAGE WHEN the Vd is large, a large loading dose may be needed at the onset ELIMINATION IS ALTERED BY DISEASE of therapy. This can be calculated from the following equation: Renal disease or reduced cardiac output often reduces the clear- VVd Desired plasma concentration (5) ance of drugs that depend on renal function. Alteration of Loading dose = Bioavailability clearance by liver disease is less common but may also occur. Impairment of hepatic clearance occurs (for high extraction drugs) Note that clearance does not enter into this computation. If when liver blood flow is reduced, as in heart failure, and in severe the loading dose is large (Vd much larger than blood volume), the cirrhosis and other forms of liver failure. The dosage in a patient dose should be given slowly to prevent toxicity due to excessively with renal impairment may be corrected by multiplying the aver- high plasma levels during the distribution phase. age dosage for a normal person times the ratio of the patient’s 28 PART I Basic Principles altered creatinine clearance (CLcr) to normal creatinine clearance QUESTIONS (approximately 100 mL/min, or 6 L/h). Questions 1–2. Mr Jones is admitted to the hospital with cough, shortness of breath, and fever. History, physical examination, and Patient’s CL cr (6) culture of the sputum lead to a diagnosis of pneumonia due to Corrected dosage = AverageAverage dosagedosage × 100 mL/min gram-negative bacteria. The antibiotic tobramycin is ordered. The clearance and Vd of tobramycin in Mr Jones are 80 mL/min and 40 L, respectively. This simplified approach ignores nonrenal routes of clearance that may be significant. If a drug is cleared partly by the kidney 1. What maintenance dose should be administered intrave- and partly by other routes, Equation 6 should be applied to the nously every 6 h to eventually obtain average steady-state a drug is 50% cleared by the kidney and 50% by the liver and (A) 0.32 mg (B) 19.2 mgthe normal dosage is 200 mg/d, the hepatic and renal elimination (C) 115 mg rates are each 100 mg/d. Therefore, the corrected dosage in a (D) 160 mg patient with a creatinine clearance of 20 mL/min will be: (E) 230 mg Dosage = 100100 mg/dg/d (li(liver)e ) + 100 mg/d 2. If you wish to give Mr Jones an intravenous loading dose to achieve the therapeutic plasma concentration of 4 mg/L × (kidney) (7) (A) 0.1 mg 100 mL/min (B) 10 mg Dosage = 100100 mg/dg/d + 20 mg/ d = 120 mg/d (C) 115.2 mg (D) 160 mg (E) None of the above Renal function is altered by many diseases and is often decreased"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","long_stem_manual_review","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-08893496ac23","stem":"A 50-year-old woman with metastatic breast cancer has 7. Your patient has been receiving lidocaine for 8 h, and the elected to participate in the trial of a new chemotherapeutic arrhythmia is suppressed. However, there are some signs of agent. It is given by constant intravenous infusion of 8 mg/h. toxicity. You decide to obtain a plasma concentration mea- Plasma concentrations (Cp) are measured with the results surement. When the results come back, the plasma level is shown in the following table. exactly twice what you expected. How should the infusion rate be modified?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-cd659c75fdfc","stem":"A 63-year-old woman in the intensive care unit requires an 4 2.0 infusion of procainamide. Its half-life is 2 h. The infusion is 8 3.0 begun at 9 AM. At 1 PM on the same day, a blood sample is taken; the drug concentration is found to be 3 mg/L. What 10 3.6 is the probable steady-state drug concentration, for example, after 12 or more hours of infusion?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b29f00254c68","stem":"A 30-year-old man is brought to the emergency department 40 4.0 in a deep coma. Respiration is severely depressed and he has pinpoint pupils. His friends state that he self-administered a large dose of morphine 6 h earlier. An immediate blood analy- What conclusion can be drawn from these data? sis shows a morphine blood level of 0.25 mg/L. Assuming that (A) Volume of distribution is 30 L the Vd of morphine in this patient is 200 L and the half-life is (B) Clearance is 2 L/h 3 h, how much morphine did the patient inject 6 h earlier? (C) Elimination follows zero-order kinetics (A) 25 mg (D) Half-life is 8 h (B) 50 mg (E) Doubling the rate of infusion would result in a plasma (C) 100 mg concentration of 16 mg/L at 40 h (D) 200 mg (E) Not enough data to predict","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-10829efba29c","stem":"You are the only physician in a clinic that is cut off from the outside world by violent storms and flooding. A 19-year-old 10. Gentamicin, an aminoglycoside antibiotic, is sometimes given woman is brought to the clinic with severe asthmatic wheez- in intermittent intravenous bolus doses of 100 mg 3 times a day ing. Because of the lack of other drugs, you decide to use to achieve target peak plasma concentrations of about 5 mg/L. intravenous theophylline for treatment. The pharmacokinetics Gentamicin’s clearance (normally 5.4 L/h/70 kg) is almost of theophylline include the following average parameters: Vd entirely by glomerular filtration. Your patient, however, is 35 L; CL 48 mL/min; half-life 8 h. If an intravenous infu- found to have a creatinine clearance one third of normal. What sion of theophylline is started at a rate of 0.48 mg/min, how should your modified dosage regimen for this patient be? long would it take to reach 93.75% of the final steady-state (A) 20 mg 3 times a day concentration? (B) 33 mg 3 times a day (A) Approximately 48 min (C) 72 mg 3 times a day (B) Approximately 7.4 h (D) 100 mg 2 times a day (C) Approximately 8 h (E) 150 mg 2 times a day (D) Approximately 24 h (E) Approximately 32 h ANSWERS","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Pharmacokinetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8cdd6235aa2b","stem":"A continuous intravenous infusion of lidocaine, 1.92 mg/min, Rate in = Rate out at steady state is started at 8 AM. The average pharmacokinetic parameters of lidocaine are: Vd 77 L; clearance 640 mL/min; half-life 1.4 h. Plasmam levelss Clearance What is the expected steady-state plasma concentration?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-daaeb296ee6d","stem":"92 mg/min = CpC ss × CL liver before it enters the systemic circulation for distribution to the body. Metabolism by enzymes in any of these tissues, expulsion by drug transporters, and excretion into the bile all may contribute to the first-pass effect of oral administration. CHAPTER 3 Pharmacokinetics 31 C H E C K L I S T When you complete this chapter, you should be able to: ❑ Estimate clearance and volume of distribution from a table or graph of plasma concentrations of a drug over time following a single known dose. ❑ Estimate the half-life of a drug based on its clearance and volume of distribution or from a graph of its plasma concentration over time. ❑ Calculate loading and maintenance dosage regimens for oral or intravenous administration of a drug when given the following information: minimum therapeutic concentration, oral bioavailability, clearance, and volume of distribution. ❑ Calculate the dosage adjustment required for a patient with impaired renal function. CHAPTER 3 Summary Table Major Concept Description Loading dose The dose required to achieve a specific plasma drug concentration level (Cp) with a single administration. Because this requires filling the volume of distribution (Vd), the calculation uses the volume of distribution (Vd) equation as: Loading dose = Cp(target) × Vd; has units of mg Maintenance dose The dose required for regular administration to maintain a target plasma level. Because this requires restoring the amount of drug lost to elimination (clearance, CL), the calculation uses the clearance equation as: Maintenance dose = Cp(target) × CL; has units of mg per time Half-life The half-life concept is useful in predicting the time course of falling drug levels after administration is stopped, and in predicting the time course of increase in drug level when repeated administration is begun—see Figure 3–3 Therapeutic window The therapeutic window is much more useful as a clinical measure of drug safety and as a guide to dosage than the older therapeutic index. The classic therapeutic index, TI, determined from animal measures of therapeutically effective dosage and lethal dosage, is inapplicable to human therapeutics, whereas the minimum therapeutic dosage and the minimum toxic dosage is readily determined in clinical trials This page intentionally left blank C H A P T E R Drug Metabolism 4 All organisms are exposed to foreign chemical compounds terminate the action of many endogenous substances, evolution (xenobiotics) in the air, water, and food. To ensure elimina- has provided metabolic pathways that alter their activity and tion of pharmacologically active xenobiotics as well as to their susceptibility to excretion. Drug Metabolism Phase I Phase II Genetic Induction Inhibition reactions reactions factors of drug of drug metabolism metabolism THE NEED FOR DRUG METABOLISM mechanisms for excreting undesirable substances produced within the body. Biotransformation of drugs is one such process. It is an Many cells that act as portals for entry of external molecules important mechanism by which the body terminates the action into the body (eg, pulmonary epithelium, intestinal epithelium) of many drugs. In some cases, it serves to activate prodrugs. Most contain transporter molecules (MDR family [P-glycoproteins], drugs are relatively lipid soluble as given, a characteristic needed MRP family, others) that expel unwanted molecules immediately for absorption across membranes. The same property would result after absorption. However, some foreign molecules evade these in very slow removal from the body because the unchanged gatekeepers and are absorbed. Therefore, all higher organisms, molecule would also be readily reabsorbed from the urine in the especially terrestrial animals, require mechanisms for ridding them- renal tubule. The body hastens excretion by transforming many selves of toxic foreign molecules after they are absorbed, as well as drugs to less lipid-soluble, less readily reabsorbed forms. High-Yield Terms to Learn Phase I reactions Reactions that convert the parent drug to a more polar (water-soluble) or more reactive product by unmasking or inserting a polar functional group such as —OH, —SH, or —NH2 Phase II reactions Reactions that increase water solubility by conjugation of the drug molecule with a polar moiety such as glucuronate, acetate, or sulfate CYP isozymes Cytochrome P450 enzyme species (eg, CYP2D and CYP3A4) that are responsible for much of drug metabolism. Many isoforms of CYP have been recognized Enzyme induction Stimulation of drug-metabolizing capacity; usually manifested in the liver by increased synthe- sis of smooth endoplasmic reticulum (which contains high concentrations of phase I enzymes) P-glycoprotein, MDR-1 An ATP-dependent transport molecule found in many epithelial and cancer cells. The transporter expels drug molecules from the cytoplasm into the extracellular space. In epithelial cells, expulsion is via the external or luminal face 33 34 PART I Basic Principles TABLE 4–1 Examples of phase I drug-metabolizing reactions. Reaction Type Typical Drug Substrates Oxidations, P450 dependent Hydroxylation Amphetamines, barbiturates, phenytoin, warfarin N-dealkylation Caffeine, morphine, theophylline O-dealkylation Codeine N-oxidation Acetaminophen, nicotine S-oxidation Chlorpromazine, cimetidine, thioridazine Deamination Amphetamine, diazepam Oxidations, P450 independent Amine oxidation Epinephrine Dehydrogenation Chloral hydrate, ethanol Reductions Chloramphenicol, clonazepam, dantrolene, naloxone Hydrolyses Esters Aspirin, clofibrate, procaine, succinylcholine Amides Indomethacin, lidocaine, procainamide TYPES OF METABOLIC REACTIONS optical enantiomers, in particular, are often metabolized at dif- ferent rates.","type":"Calculation","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Pharmacokinetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phase I Reactions"},{"label":"B","text":"Phase II Reactions Phase I reactions include oxidation (especially by the cyto- chrome P450 group of enzymes, also called mixed-function Phase II reactions are synthetic reactions that involve addition oxidases), reduction, deamination, and hydrolysis. Examples are (conjugation) of subgroups to —OH, —NH2, and —SH functions listed in Table 4–1. These enzymes are found in high concentra- on the drug molecule. The subgroups that are added include gluc- tions in the smooth endoplasmic reticulum of the liver. They uronate, acetate, glutathione, glycine, sulfate, and methyl groups. are not highly selective in their substrates, so a relatively small Most of these groups are relatively polar and make the product less number of P450 isoforms are able to metabolize thousands of lipid-soluble than the original drug molecule. Examples of phase II drugs. Of the drugs metabolized by phase I cytochrome P450s, reactions are listed in Table 4–2. Like phase I enzymes, phase II approximately 75% are metabolized by just two: CYP3A4 or enzymes are not very selective. Drugs that are metabolized by both CYP2D6. Nevertheless, some selectivity can be detected, and routes may undergo phase II metabolism before or after phase I. TABLE 4–2 Examples of phase II drug-metabolizing reactions. Reaction Type Typical Drug Substrates Glucuronidation Acetaminophen, diazepam, digoxin, morphine, sulfamethiazole Acetylation Clonazepam, dapsone, isoniazid, mescaline, sulfonamides Glutathione conjugation Ethacrynic acid, reactive phase I metabolite of acetaminophen Glycine conjugation Deoxycholic acid, nicotinic acid (niacin), salicylic acid Sulfation Acetaminophen, methyldopa Methylation Dopamine, epinephrine, histamine, norepinephrine, thiouracil Adapted, with permission, from Katzung BG, editor: Basic & Clinical Pharmacology, 12th ed. McGraw-Hill, 2012. CHAPTER 4 Drug Metabolism 35 SITES OF DRUG METABOLISM (“pseudocholinesterase” or butyrylcholinesterase). In most per- sons, this process occurs very rapidly, and a single dose of this The most important organ for drug metabolism is the liver. The neuromuscular-blocking drug has a duration of action of about kidneys play an important role in the metabolism of some drugs. A 5 min. Approximately 1 person in 2500 has an abnormal form few drugs (eg, esters) are metabolized in many tissues (eg, liver, blood, of this enzyme that metabolizes succinylcholine and similar esters intestinal wall) because of the wide distribution of their enzymes. much more slowly. In such persons, the neuromuscular paralysis produced by a single dose of succinylcholine may last many hours. DETERMINANTS OF 2. Acetylation of amines—Isoniazid and some other amines BIOTRANSFORMATION RATE such as hydralazine and procainamide are metabolized in a phase II reaction by N-acetylation. People who are deficient in The rate of biotransformation of a drug may vary markedly among acetylation capacity, termed slow acetylators, may have prolonged different individuals. This variation is most