es","subtopic":"Enzyme","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"B","text":"Effects of Other Drugs"},{"label":"A","text":"Genetic Factors Coadministration of certain agents may alter the disposition of Because recent advances in genomic techniques are making it many drugs. Mechanisms include the following: possible to screen for a huge variety of polymorphisms, it is expected that pharmacogenomics will become an important part 1. Enzyme induction—Induction (increased rate and extent of patient evaluation in the future, influencing both drug choice of metabolism) usually results from increased synthesis of cyto- and drug dosing. Several drug-metabolizing systems have already chrome P450-dependent drug-oxidizing enzymes in the liver as been shown to differ among families or populations in genetically well as the cofactor, heme. Several cytoplasmic drug receptors determined ways. However, screening for these variants has not have been identified that result in activation of the genes for P450 yet become common. isoforms. Many isozymes of the P450 family exist, and inducers selectively increase subgroups of isozymes. Common inducers of a"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Proteins and Enzymes","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b8ea4946c1d6","stem":"Which of the following drugs may inhibit the hepatic micro-(eg, acetylcysteine) may be life-saving after an overdose. In severe somal P450 responsible for warfarin metabolism? liver disease, stores of glucuronide, sulfate, and glutathione may (A) Amiodarone be depleted, making the patient more susceptible to hepatic toxic- (B) Ethanol ity with near-normal doses of acetaminophen. Enzyme inducers (C) Phenobarbital (eg, ethanol) may increase acetaminophen toxicity because they (D) Procainamide increase phase I metabolism more than phase II metabolism, thus (E) Rifampin resulting in increased production of the reactive metabolite. 7. Which of the following drugs, if used chronically, is most likely to increase the toxicity of acetaminophen? (A) Cimetidine QUESTIONS (B) Ethanol (C) Ketoconazole 1–2. You are planning to treat asthma in a 19-year-old patient (D) Procainamide with recurrent, episodic attacks of bronchospasm with wheezing. (E) Quinidine You are concerned about drug interactions caused by changes in (F) Ritonavir drug metabolism in this patient. (G) Succinylcholine","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["possible_multiple_questions_in_stem","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"f527b0c835b85d49aa90603ea1b34ec4c8f5f7ae"},{"id":"q-66bf3036e300","stem":"Which of the following drugs has higher first-pass metabolism (B) More likely to distribute intracellularly in men than in women?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d53483da36e5","stem":"Which of the following drugs is an established inhibitor of (D) Require 3–4 months to reach completion P-glycoprotein (P-gp) drug transporters?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bc0534b3bcf9","stem":"Which of the following factors is likely to increase the duration (B) Ethanol of action of a drug that is metabolized by CYP3A4 in the (C) Ketoconazole liver?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-86d425ae3101","stem":"Which of the following agents, when used in combination (E) Chronic administration of rifampin with other anti-HIV drugs, permits dose reductions?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e3593afcedc7","stem":"Reports of cardiac arrhythmias caused by unusually high (A) Cimetidine blood levels of 2 antihistamines, terfenadine and astemizole, (B) Efavirenz led to their removal from the market. Which of the following (C) Ketoconazole best explains these effects?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antifungal","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5159190f042d","stem":"Hydralazine, like procainamide and isoniazid, is metabolized by N-acetylation, an enzymatic process that is slow in about 20% of Asians and in about 50% of European Americans and African Americans. The answer is B. C H E C K L I S T When you complete this chapter, you should be able to: ❑ List the major phase I and phase II metabolic reactions. ❑ Describe the mechanism of hepatic enzyme induction and list 3 drugs that are known to cause it. ❑ List 3 drugs that inhibit the metabolism of other drugs. ❑ List 3 drugs for which there are well-defined, genetically determined differences in metabolism. ❑ Describe some of the effects of smoking, liver disease, and kidney disease on drug elimination. ❑ Describe the pathways by which acetaminophen is metabolized (1) to harmless products if normal doses are taken and (2) to hepatotoxic products if an overdose is taken. CHAPTER 4 Drug Metabolism 39 CHAPTER 4 Summary Table Major Concept Description Drug metabolism vs drug Termination of drug action requires either removal of the drug from the body (excretion) or modification of elimination the drug molecule (metabolism) so that it no longer has an effect. Both methods constitute drug elimination, and both are very important in the clinical use of drugs. Almost all drugs (or their metabolites) are eventually excreted, but for many, excretion occurs only some time after they have been metabolized to inactive products Induction and inhibition of A large number of drugs alter their own metabolism and the metabolism of other drugs either by drug metabolism inducing the synthesis of larger amounts of the metabolizing enzymes (usually P450 enzymes in the liver) or by inhibiting those enzymes. Some drugs both inhibit (acutely) and induce (with chronic administration) drug metabolism Pharmacogenomic variation in Genetic variations in drug metabolism undoubtedly occur for many drugs. Specific differences have been drug metabolism defined for (1) succinylcholine and similar esters, (2) procainamide and similar amines, and (3) a miscellaneous group that includes β blockers, antidepressants, and others Toxic metabolism Some substances are metabolized to toxic molecules by drug-metabolizing enzymes. Important examples include methyl alcohol, ethylene glycol, and, at high doses or in the presence of liver disease, acetaminophen. See Figure 4–1 and Chapter 23 This page intentionally left blank C H A P T E R Drug Evaluation& Regulation 5 The sale and use of drugs are regulated in almost all countries by Rational molecular design or screening is then used to find governmental agencies. In the United States, regulation is by the a molecule that selectively alters the function of the target. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). New drugs are devel- New drugs may result from the screening of hundreds of com- oped in industrial or academic laboratories. Before a new drug pounds against model diseases in animals. In contrast, many can be approved for regular therapeutic use in humans, a series (so-called “me-too” drugs) are the result of simple chemical of animal and experimental human studies must be carried out. alteration of the pharmacokinetic properties of the original, New drugs may emerge from a variety of sources. Some prototype agent. are the result of identification of a new target for a disease. Drug Evaluation and Regulation Safety,y Animal Clinical Patents, effficacy testing trials generics, legislation, orphan drugs SAFETY & EFFICACY ANIMAL TESTING Because society expects prescription drugs to be safe and effec- The animal testing that is required before human studies can tive, governments regulate the development and marketing of begin is a function of the proposed use and the urgency of the new drugs. Current regulations require evidence of relative safety application. Thus, a drug proposed for occasional nonsystemic (derived from acute and subacute toxicity testing in animals) and use requires less extensive testing than one destined for chronic probable therapeutic action (from the pharmacologic profile in systemic administration. animals) before human testing is permitted. Some information Because of the urgent need for new agents, anticancer drugs and about the pharmacokinetics of a compound is also required before anti-HIV drugs require less evidence of safety than do drugs used clinical evaluation is begun. Chronic toxicity test results are gener- in treatment of less threatening diseases. Urgently needed drugs are ally not required, but testing must be underway before human often investigated and approved on an accelerated schedule. studies are started. The development of a new drug and its path- conditions, and patients are closely monitored, often in a hospital determination of the therapeutic index of a new drug in mice? at doses that are tolerated by sick patients. Detailed data are col- maximal efficacy of the drug in humans?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Pharmacokinetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute Toxicityway through various levels of testing and regulation are illustrated in Figure 5–1. The cost of development of a new drug, includ- Acute toxicity studies are required for all new drugs. These studies ing false starts and discarded molecules, is often several hundred involve administration of incrementing doses of the agent up to million dollars. the lethal level in at least 2 species (eg, 1 rodent and 1 nonrodent). 41 42 PART I Basic Principles High-Yield Terms to Learn Mutagenic An effect on the inheritable characteristics of a cell or organism—a mutation in the DNA; usually tested in microorganisms with the Ames test Carcinogenic An effect of inducing malignant characteristics Teratogenic An effect on the in utero development of an organism resulting in abnormal structure or function; not generally heritable Placebo An inactive “dummy” medication made up to resemble the active investigational formulation as much as possible but lacking therapeutic effect Single-blind study A clinical trial in which the investigators—but not the subjects—know which subjects are receiving active drug and which are receiving placebos Double-blind study A clinical trial in which neither the subjects nor the investigators know which subjects are receiving placebos; the code is held by a third party IND Investigational New Drug Exemption; an application for FDA approval to carry out new drug trials in humans; requires animal data NDA New Drug Application; seeks FDA approval to market a new drug for ordinary clinical use. Requires data from clinical trials as well as preclinical (animal) data Phases 1, 2, and 3 of Three parts of a clinical trial that are usually carried out before submitting an NDA to the FDA clinical trials Positive control A known standard therapy, to be used along with placebo, to evaluate the superiority or inferiority of a new drug in relation to the others available Orphan drugs Drugs developed for diseases in which the expected number of patients is small. Some countries bestow certain commercial advantages on companies that develop drugs for uncommon diseases In vitro Animal Clinical testing Marketing studies testing (Is it safe, Phase 1 Generics pharmacokinetics?) Biologic become 20–100 products subjects (Does it available Phase 2 work in patients?) 100–200 Efficacy patients Phase 3 Lead compound selectivity mechanism (Does it work, double blind?) Phase 4 1000–6000 patients (Postmarketing Chemical surveillance) synthesis Drug metabolism, safety assessment 0 2 4 8–9 20 Years (average) IND NDA (Patent expires (Investigational (New Drug 20 years after filing New Drug) Application) of application) FIGURE 5–1 The development and testing process required to bring a new drug to market in the United States. Some requirements may be different for drugs used in life-threatening diseases. (Reproduced, with permission, from Katzung BG, editor: Basic & Clinical Pharmacology, 12th ed. McGraw-Hill, 2012: Fig. 5–1.) CHAPTER 5 Drug Evaluation & Regulation 43"},{"label":"B","text":"Subacute and Chronic Toxicity neonates for abnormalities. Examples of drugs known to have teratogenic effects include thalidomide, isotretinoin, valproic Subacute and chronic toxicity testing are required for most agents, acid, ethanol, glucocorticoids, warfarin, lithium, and androgens. especially those intended for chronic use. Tests are usually con- Mutagenesis is induction of changes in the genetic material of ducted for 2–4 weeks (subacute) and 6–24 months (chronic), in animals of any age and therefore induction of heritable abnormali- at least 2 species. ties. The Ames test, the standard in vitro test for mutagenicity, uses a special strain of salmonella bacteria that naturally depends on specific nutrients in the culture medium. Loss of this depen- TYPES OF ANIMAL TESTS dence as a result of exposure to the test drug signals a mutation. Many carcinogens (eg, aflatoxin, cancer chemotherapeutic drugs, Tests done with animals usually include general screening tests and other agents that bind to DNA) have mutagenic effects and for pharmacologic effects, hepatic and renal function monitoring, test positive in the Ames test. The dominant lethal test is an blood and urine tests, gross and histopathologic examination of in vivo mutagenicity test carried out in mice. Male animals are tissues, and tests of reproductive effects and carcinogenicity. exposed to the test substance before mating. Abnormalities in the results of subsequent mating (eg, loss of embryos, deformed"},{"label":"A","text":"Pharmacologic Profile fetuses) signal a mutation in the male’s germ cells. The pharmacologic profile is a description of all the pharma- cologic effects of a drug (eg, effects on cardiovascular function, C. Carcinogenesis gastrointestinal activity, respiration, renal function, endocrine Carcinogenesis is the induction of malignant characteristics infunction, CNS). Both graded and quantal dose-response data are cells. Carcinogenicity is difficult and expensive to study, andgathered. the Ames test is often used to screen chemicals because there is a moderately high degree of correlation between mutagenicity in"},{"label":"B","text":"Reproductive Toxicity the Ames test and carcinogenicity in some animal tests, as previ- Reproductive toxicity testing involves the study of the fertility ously noted. Agents with known carcinogenic effects include coal effects of the candidate drug and its teratogenic and mutagenic tar, aflatoxin, dimethylnitrosamine and other nitrosamines, ure- toxicity. The FDA uses a 5-level descriptive scale to summarize thane, vinyl chloride, and the polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons in information regarding the safety of drugs in pregnancy (Table 5–1). tobacco smoke (eg, benzo[a]pyrene) and other tobacco products. Teratogenesis can be defined as the induction of developmental defects in the somatic tissues of the fetus (eg, by exposure of the fetus to a chemical, infection, or radiation). Teratogenesis CLINICAL TRIALS is studied by treating pregnant female animals of at least 2 spe- cies at selected times during early pregnancy when organogenesis Human testing of new drugs in the United States requires is known to take place and by later examining the fetuses or approval by institutional committees that monitor the ethical TABLE 5–1 FDA ratings of drug safety in pregnancy. Category Description A Controlled studies in women fail to demonstrate a risk to the fetus in the first trimester (and there is no evidence of a risk in later trimesters), and the possibility of fetal harm appears remote B Either animal reproduction studies have not demonstrated a fetal risk but there are no controlled studies in pregnant women, or animal reproduction studies have shown an adverse effect (other than a decrease in fertility) that was not confirmed in controlled studies in women in the first trimester (and there is no evidence of a risk in later trimesters) C Either studies in animals have revealed adverse effects on the fetus (teratogenic or embryocidal or other) and there are no controlled studies in women, or studies in women and animals are not available. Drugs should be given only when the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus D There is positive evidence of human fetal risk, but the benefits from use in pregnant women may be acceptable despite the risk (eg, if the drug is needed in a life-threatening situation or for a serious disease for which safer drugs cannot be used or are ineffective) X Studies in animals or human beings have demonstrated fetal abnormalities or there is evidence of fetal risk based on human experience or both, and the risk of the use of the drug in pregnant women clearly outweighs any possible benefit. The drug is contraindicated in women who are or may become pregnant 44 PART I Basic Principles (informed consent, patient safety) and scientific aspects (study D. Phase 4 design, statistical power) of the proposed tests. Such testing also Phase 4 represents the postmarketing surveillance phase of requires the prior approval by the FDA of an Investigational evaluation, in which it is hoped that toxicities that occur very New Drug Exemption application (IND), which is submitted infrequently will be detected and reported early enough to pre- by the manufacturer to the FDA (Figure 5–1). The IND includes vent major therapeutic disasters. Manufacturers are required to all the preclinical data collected up to the time of submission and inform the FDA at regular intervals of all reported untoward drug the detailed proposal for clinical trials. The major clinical testing reactions. Unlike the first 3 phases, phase 4 has not been rigidly process is usually divided into 3 phases that are carried out to regulated by the FDA in the past. Because so many drugs have provide information for a New Drug Application (NDA). The been found to be unacceptably toxic only after they have been NDA includes all the results of preclinical and clinical testing and marketed, there is considerable current interest in making phase 4 constitutes the request for FDA approval of general marketing of surveillance more consistent, effective, and informative. the new agent for prescription use. A fourth phase of study (the surveillance phase) follows NDA approval. DRUG PATENTS & GENERIC DRUGS"},{"label":"A","text":"Phase 1 A patent application is usually submitted around the time that A phase 1 trial consists of careful evaluation of the dose-response a new drug enters animal testing. In the United States, approval relationship and the pharmacokinetics of the new drug in a small of the patent and completion of the NDA approval process give number of normal human volunteers (eg, 20–100). An excep- the originator the right to market the drug without competition tion is the phase 1 trials of canc