a double-blind crossover design. The goals are to explore further, under the conditions of the proposed clinical use, ORPHAN DRUGS the spectrum of beneficial actions of the new drug, to compare it with older therapies, and to discover toxicities, if any, that occur An orphan drug is a drug for a rare disease (one affecting fewer so infrequently as to be undetectable in phase 2 studies. Very than 200,000 people in the United States). The study of such large amounts of data are collected and these studies are usually agents has often been neglected because the sales of an effective very expensive. agent for an uncommon ailment might not pay the costs of devel- If the drug successfully completes phase 3, an NDA is submitted opment. In the United States, current legislation provides for tax to the FDA. If the NDA is approved, the drug can be marketed relief and other incentives designed to encourage the development and phase 4 begins. of orphan drugs. CHAPTER 5 Drug Evaluation & Regulation 45 TABLE 5–2 Selected legislation pertaining to drugs in the United States. Law Purpose and Effect Pure Food and Drug Act of 1906 Prohibited mislabeling and adulteration of foods and drugs (but no requirement for efficacy or safety) Harrison Narcotics Act of 1914 Established regulations for the use of opium, opioids, and cocaine (marijuana added in 1937) Food, Drug, and Cosmetics Act of 1938 Required that new drugs be tested for safety as well as purity Kefauver-Harris Amendment (1962) Required proof of efficacy as well as safety for new drugs Dietary Supplement and Health Education Amended the Food, Drug, and Cosmetics act of 1938 to establish standards for dietary supplements but Act (1994) prohibited the FDA from applying drug efficacy and safety standards to supplements QUESTIONS 5. Which of the following statements about the testing of new compounds for potential therapeutic use in the treatment of"}],"priority":2,"status":"quarantine","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_ques