ts on adrenergic transmission—Drugs that presynaptic nerve terminals, and exocrine glands). Evidence block norepinephrine synthesis (eg, metyrosine) or catechol- (including their genes) has been found for 5 subtypes, of which amine storage (eg, reserpine) or release (eg, guanethidine) were 3 appear to be important in peripheral autonomic transmission. used in treatment of several diseases (eg, hypertension) because All 5 are G-protein-coupled receptors (see Chapter 2). they block sympathetic but not parasympathetic functions. Other drugs promote catecholamine release (eg, the amphet- 2. Nicotinic receptors—These receptors are located on amine-like agents) and predictably cause sympathomimetic Na+-K+ ion channels and respond to acetylcholine and nicotine, effects. another acetylcholine mimic (but not to muscarine) by open- ing the channel. The 2 major nicotinic subtypes are located in","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"C","text":"Cotransmitters ganglia and in skeletal muscle end plates. The nicotinic recep- tors are the primary receptors for transmission at these sites. Many (probably all) autonomic nerves have transmitter vesicles that contain other transmitter molecules in addition to the primary"},{"label":"B","text":"Adrenoceptorsagents (acetylcholine or norepinephrine) previously described. These cotransmitters may be localized in the same vesicles as Also referred to as adrenergic receptors, adrenoceptors are divided the primary transmitter or in a separate population of vesicles. into several subtypes (Table 6–2). Substances recognized to date as cotransmitters include ATP (adenosine triphosphate), enkephalins, vasoactive intestinal 1. Alpha receptors—These are located on vascular smooth peptide, neuropeptide Y, substance P, neurotensin, somatosta- muscle, presynaptic nerve terminals, blood platelets, fat cells tin, and others. Their main role in autonomic function appears to (lipocytes), and neurons in the brain. Alpha receptors are further involve modulation of synaptic transmission. The same substances divided into 2 major types, α1 and α2. These 2 subtypes con- function as primary transmitters in other synapses. stitute different families and use different G-coupling proteins. RECEPTOR CHARACTERISTICS 2. Beta receptors—These receptors are located on most types of smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, some presynaptic nerve terminals, and lipocytes. Beta receptors are divided into 3 majorThe major receptor systems in the ANS include cholinoceptors, subtypes, β1, β2, and β3. These subtypes are rather similar andadrenoceptors, and dopamine receptors, which have been studied use the same G-coupling protein.in some detail. The numerous receptors for cotransmitter sub- stances have not been as fully defined."},{"label":"C","text":"Dopamine Receptors"},{"label":"A","text":"Cholinoceptors Dopamine (D, DA) receptors are a subclass of adrenoceptors Also referred to as cholinergic receptors, these molecules respond but with rather different distribution and function. Dopamine to acetylcholine and its analogs. Cholinoceptors are subdivided as receptors are especially important in the renal and splanchnic follows (Table 6–1): vessels and in the brain. Although at least 5 subtypes exist, the D1 subtype appears to be the most important dopamine receptor"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5cfaaed0263b","stem":"Hydroxydopamine Adrenergic nerve terminals Destroy