:"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"B","text":"Systemic Reflexes determined by the vasomotor center and renal sensors. Any devia- System reflexes regulate blood pressure, gastrointestinal motil- tion from this blood pressure “set point” causes a change in ANS ity, bladder tone, airway smooth muscle, and other processes. activity and renin-angiotensin-aldosterone levels. For example, a CHAPTER 6 Introduction to Autonomic Pharmacology 55 Noradrenergic nerve terminal Release-modulating NE receptors M − AT1 + α2 − Uptake 1 NE (NET) FIGURE 6–3 Local control of autonomic nervous system function via modu- Negative NE feedback lation of transmitter release. In the example shown, release of norepinephrine (NE) from a sympathetic nerve ending is modulated by norepinephrine itself, β Adrenoceptor acting on presynaptic α2 autoreceptors, and by acetylcholine and angiotensin II, acting on heteroreceptors. Many other modulators (see text) influence Cardiac muscle cell the release process. AT1, angiotensin II receptor; M, muscarinic receptor; (sinoatrial node) NET, norepinephrine transporter. VASOMOTOR CENTER Sympathetic autonomic Parasympathetic nervous autonomic system nervous loop system Baroreceptors – + + + Autonomic + feedback Peripheral Heart Contractile Venous vascular rate force tone resistance Mean Cardiac Stroke Venous Blood arterial output volume return volume pressure loop Aldosterone Hormonal feedback Renal blood Renin Angiotensin flow/pressure FIGURE 6–4 Autonomic and hormonal control of cardiovascular function. Note that 2 feedback loops are present: the autonomic nervous system loop and the hormonal loop. Each major loop has several components. In the neuronal loop, sensory input to the vasomotor center is via afferent fibers in the ninth and tenth cranial (PANS) nerves. On the efferent side, the sympathetic nervous system directly influences 4 major variables: peripheral vascular resistance, heart rate, contractile force, and venous tone. The parasympathetic nervous system directly influences heart rate. In addition, angiotensin II directly increases peripheral vascular resistance (not shown), and sympathetic nervous system discharge directly increases renin secretion (not shown). Because these control mechanisms have evolved to maintain normal blood pressure, the net feedback effect of each loop is negative; feedback tends to compensate for the change in arterial blood pressure that evoked the response. Thus, decreased blood pressure due to blood loss would be compensated by increased sympathetic outflow and renin release. Conversely, elevated pressure due to the administration of a vasoconstrictor drug would cause reduced sympathetic outflow, decreased renin release, and increased parasympathetic (vagal) outflow. (Modified and reproduced, with permission, from Katzung BG, editor: Basic & Clinical Pharmacology, 12th ed. McGraw-Hill, 2012: Fig. 6–7.) 56 PART II Autonomic Drugs Cornea Canal of Schlemm Anterior chamber Trabecular meshwork Dilator (α) Sphincter (M) Sclera Iris Lens Ciliary epithelium (β) Ciliary muscle (M) FIGURE 6–5 Some pharmacologic targets in the eye. The diagram illustrates clinically important structures and their receptors. The heavy arrow (blue) illustrates the flow of aqueous humor from its secretion by the ciliary epithelium to its drainage through the canal of Schlemm. M, muscarinic receptor; α, alpha receptor; β, beta receptor. (Modified and reproduced, with permission, from Katzung BG, editor: Basic & Clinical Pharmacology, 12th ed. McGraw-Hill, 2012: Fig. 6–9.) decrease in blood pressure caused by hemorrhage causes increased accommodation, is under primary control of muscarinic receptors SANS discharge and renin release. As a result, peripheral vascular innervated by the PANS, with insignificant contributions from resistance, venous tone, heart rate, and cardiac force are increased the SANS. The ciliary epithelium, on the other hand, has impor- by norepinephrine released from sympathetic nerves. This ANS tant β receptors that have a permissive effect on aqueous humor response can be blocked with ganglion-blocking drugs such as secretion. Each of these receptors is an important target of drugs hexamethonium. Blood volume is replenished by retention of that are discussed in the following chapters. salt and water in the kidney under the influence of increased levels of aldosterone. These compensatory responses may be large enough to overcome some of the actions of drugs. For example, QUESTIONS the chronic treatment of hypertension with a vasodilator such as 1. A 3-year-old child has swallowed the contents of 2 bottles of hydralazine will be unsuccessful when the compensatory tachycar- a nasal decongestant whose primary ingredient is a potent, dia (via the baroreceptor reflex) and the salt and water retention selective α-adrenoceptor agonist drug. Which of the follow- (via the renin system response) are not prevented through the use ing is a sign of α-receptor activation that may occur in this (A) Bronchodilation (B) Cardiac acceleration (tachycardia)"},{"label":"C","text":"Complex Organ Control: The Eye (C) Pupillary dilation (mydriasis) (D) Renin release from the kidneys The eye contains multiple tissues, several of them under auto- (E) Vasodilation of the splanchnic vessels nomic control (Figure 6–5). The pupil, discussed previously, is under reciprocal control by the SANS (via α receptors on the pupillary dilator muscle) and the PANS (via muscarinic receptors on the pupillary constrictor). The ciliary muscle, which controls CHAPTER 6 Introduction to Autonomic Pharmacology 57"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrust