ishing the 2 conditions may be difficult. Administration of a muscle strength in myasthenic crisis but weaken it in cholinergic (A) It is inactivated by conversion to paraoxon crisis. (B) It is less toxic to humans than malathion (C) It is more persistent in the environment than DDT (D) It is poorly absorbed through skin and lungs"},{"label":"E","text":"Toxicity (E) If treated early, its toxicity may be partly reversed by In addition to their therapeutic uses, some indirect-acting agents pralidoxime (especially organophosphates) have clinical importance because of accidental exposures to toxic amounts of pesticides. The most toxic of these drugs (eg, parathion) can be rapidly fatal if exposure is not immediately recognized and treated. After standard protec- tion of vital signs (see Chapter 58), the antidote of first choice 66 PART II Autonomic Drugs"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","long_stem_manual_review","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-a77adb1445c2","stem":"Ms Brown has been treated for myasthenia gravis for several 9. A 3-year-old child is admitted after taking a drug from her years. She reports to the emergency department complain- parents’ medicine cabinet. The signs suggest that the drug ing of recent onset of weakness of her hands, diplopia, and is an indirect-acting cholinomimetic with little or no CNS difficulty swallowing. She may be suffering from a change in effect and a duration of action of about 2–4 h. Which of the response to her m