hemical antagonist that binds the phosphorus of organophosphates and displaces acetylcholinesterase Cycloplegia Paralysis of accommodation; inability to focus on close objects Depolarizing blockade Flaccid skeletal muscle paralysis caused by persistent depolarization of the neuromuscular end plate Miotic A drug that constricts the pupil Mydriatic A drug that dilates the pupil Nondepolarizing blockade Flaccid skeletal muscle paralysis caused by blockade of the nicotinic receptor and preven- tion of end plate depolarization Parasympatholytic, A drug that reduces the effects of parasympathetic nerve stimulation, usually by blockade parasympathoplegic of the muscarinic receptors of autonomic effector tissues bladder, and bronchi are often selected for minimum CNS activ- D. Clinical Uses ity; these drugs may incorporate quaternary amine groups to limit The muscarinic blockers have several useful therapeutic applica- penetration through the blood–brain barrier. tions in the CNS, eye, bronchi, gut, and urinary bladder. These uses are listed in the Drug Summary Table at the end of this","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Pharmacokinetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"B","text":"Mechanism of Action chapter. Although several are inverse agonists, muscarinic blocking agents act like competitive (surmountable) pharmacologic antagonists; 1. CNS—Scopolamine is standard therapy for motion sickness; their blocking effects can be overcome by increased concentrations it is one of the most effective agents available for this condi- of muscarinic agonists. tion. A transdermal patch formulation is available. Benztropine, biperiden, and trihexyphenidyl are representative of several"},{"label":"C","text":"Effects antimuscarinic agents used in parkinsonism. Although not as effective as levodopa (see Chapter 28), these agents may be The peripheral actions of muscarinic blockers are mostly predict- useful as adjuncts or when patients become unresponsive to able effects derived from cholinoceptor blockade (Table 8–1). levodopa. Benztropine is sometimes used parenterally to treat These include the ocular, gastrointestinal, genitourinary, and acute dystonias caused by antipsychotic medications. secretory effects. The CNS effects are less predictable. CNS effects seen at therapeutic concentrations include sedation, reduction of"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","long_stem_manual_review","mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions"],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-82a4302703d2","stem":"Predictable toxicities—Antimuscarinic actions lead to","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","options":[{"label":"F","text":"Contraindicationsseveral important and potentially dangerous effects. Blockade of thermoregulatory sweating may result in hyperthermia The antimuscarinic agents should be used cautiously in infants or “atropine fever.” This is the most dangerous effect of the because of the danger of hyperthermia. The drugs are relatively antimuscarinic drugs in children and is potentially lethal in contraindicated in persons with glaucoma, especially the closed- infants. Sweating, salivation, and lacrimation are all significantly angle form, and in men with prostatic hyperplasia. 74 PART II Autonomic Drugs NICOTINIC ANTAGONISTS ganglion-blocking drugs include dry mouth, blurred vision, con- stipation, and severe sexual dysfunction (Table 8–2). As a result,"},{"label":"A","text":"Ganglion-Blocking Drugs ganglion blockers are rarely used. Blockers of ganglionic nicotinic receptors act like competitive"},{"label":"B","text":"Neuromuscular-Blocking Drugs pharmacologic antagonists, although there is evidence that some also block the pore of the nicotinic channel itself. These drugs Neuromuscular-blocking drugs are important for producing were the first successful agents for the treatment of hyperten- complete skeletal muscle relaxation in surgery; new ones are intro- sion. Hexamethonium (C6, a prototype), mecamylamine, and duced regularly. They are discussed in greater detail in Chapter 27. several other ganglion blockers were extensively used for this disease. Unfortunately, the adverse effects of ganglion blockade in CHOLINESTERASE REGENERATORShypertension are so severe (both sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions are blocked) that patients were unable to tolerate them Pralidoxime is the prototype cholinesterase regenerator. These for long periods (Table 8–2). Trimethaphan was the ganglion chemical antagonists contain an oxime group, which has an blocker most recently used in clinical practice, but it too has been extremely high affinity for the phosphorus atom in organo- almost abandoned. It is poorly lipid-soluble, inactive orally, and phosphate insecticides. Because the affinity of the oxime group has a short half-life. It was used intravenously to treat severe accel- for phosphorus exceeds the affinity of the enzyme-active site for erated hypertension (malignant hypertension) and to produce phosphorus, these agents are able to bind the inhibitor and dis- controlled hypotension. place the enzyme (if aging has not occurred). The active enzyme Recent interest has focused on nicotinic receptors in the is thus regenerated. Pralidoxime, the oxime currently available CNS and their relation to nicotine addiction and to Tourette’s in the United States, may be used to treat patients exposed to syndrome. Paradoxically, nicotine (in the form of nicotine gum insecticides, such as parathion, or to nerve gases. or patches), varenicline (a partial agonist given by mouth), and mecamylamine, a nicotinic ganglion blocker that enters the CNS, have all been shown to have some benefit in smoking cessation. QUESTIONS Because ganglion blockers interrupt sympathetic control of venous tone, they cause marked venous pooling; postural hypo- Questions 1–2. A 2-year-old child has been admitted to the emer- gency department. Antimuscarinic drug overdose is suspected.tension is a major manifestation of this effect. Other toxicities of"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-e2e20d4ed04e","stem":"Drug X caused a 50 mm Hg rise in mean blood pressure in (E) Increased sodium influx into the skeletal muscle end the control animal, no blood pressure change in the ganglion- plate blocked animal, and a 75 mm mean blood pressure rise in the atropine-pretreated animal. Drug X is probably a drug similar to 9. Which one of the following drugs causes vasodilation that can (A) Acetylcholine be blocked by atropine?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic","possible_web_capture_contamination"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["possible_web_capture_contamination","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e854ba9e28a4","stem":"Which one of the following drugs has a very high affinity for +50 the phosphorus atom in parathion and is often used to treat life-threatening insecticide toxicity?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1a1633bc36c8","stem":"A 30-year-old man has been treated with several autonomic drops in children. The answer is D. drugs for 4 weeks. He is now admitted to the emergency 3. Atropine blocks muscarinic receptors and inhibits parasym- department showing signs of drug toxicity. Which of the pathomimetic effects. Nicotine can induce both parasym- following signs would distinguish between an overdose of a pathomimetic and sympathomimetic effects by virtue of ganglion blocker versus a muscarinic blocker?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-561cdc54137e","stem":"Atrial fibrillation and other arrhythmias are not responsive to P/U = antilog (2) antimuscarinic agents. The answer is A. = 100/1 8. Muscarinic M1 and M3 receptors mediate increases in IP3 and DAG in target tissues (intestine, salivary glands). M2 receptors Therefore, about 99% of the drug is in the protonated form, (heart) mediate a decrease in cAMP and an increase in potas- 1% in the unprotonated form. Since atropine is a weak base, sium permeability. Antimuscarinic agents block these effects. it is the unprotonated form that is lipid soluble. Therefore, The answer is B. about 1% of the atropine in the urine is lipid soluble. C H E C K L I S T When you complete this chapter, you should be able to: ❑ Describe the effects of atropine on the major organ systems (CNS, eye, heart, vessels, bronchi, gut, genitourinary tract, exocrine glands, skeletal muscle). ❑ List the signs, symptoms, and treatment of atropine overdose. ❑ List the major clinical indications and contraindications for the use of muscarinic antagonists. ❑ Describe the effects of the ganglion-blocking nicotinic antagonists. ❑ List one antimuscarinic agent promoted for each of the following uses: to produce mydriasis and cycloplegia; to treat parkinsonism, asthma, bladder spasm, and the muscarinic toxicity of insecticides. ❑ Describe the mechanism of action and clinical use of pralidoxime. CHAPTER 8 Cholinoceptor Blockers & Cholinesterase Regenerators 77 DRUG SUMMARY TABLE: Cholinoceptor Blockers & Cholinesterase Regenerators Mechanism of Clinical Toxicities, Subclass Action Applications Pharmacokinetics Interactions Antimuscarinic, nonselective Atropine Competitive pharmaco- Mydriatic, cycloplegic • Lipid-soluble All parasympatho- logic antagonist at all antidote for cholinester- Duration: 2–4 h lytic effects plus M receptors ase inhibitor toxicity except in eye: ≥72 h sedation, delirium, hyperthermia, flushing Benztropine, others: antiparkinsonism; oral and parenteral Dicyclomine, glycopyrrolate: oral, parenteral for gastrointestinal applications Homatropine, cyclopentolate, tropicamide: topical ophthalmic use to produce mydriasis, cycloplegia Ipratropium, tiotropium: inhaled for asthma, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease Oxybutynin: oral, transdermal, promoted for urinary urgency, incontinence Scopolamine: anti-motion sickness via transdermal patch Trospium: oral, for urinary urgency Antimuscarinic, selective Darifenacin, fesoterodine, Like atropine, but Urinary urgency, Oral Excessive solifenacin, tolterodine modest selectivity for incontinence Duration: 12–24 h parasympatholytic M3 receptors effects Pirenzepine, telenzepine Significant M1 selectivity Peptic disease (not Oral Excessive available in USA) parasympatholytic effects Antinicotinic ganglion blockers Hexamethonium Selective block of NN Obsolete; was used for Oral, parenteral Block of all receptors hypertension autonomic effects Trimethaphan: IV only, short-acting; was used for hypertensive emergencies and controlled hypotension Mecamylamine: oral, enters CNS; investigational use for smoking cessation Antinicotinic neuromuscular blockers See Chapter 27 Regenerator Pralidoxime Chemical antagonist of Organophosphate Parenteral Muscle weakness organophosphates poisoning This page intentionally left blank C H A P T E Sympathomimetics R9 The sympathomimetics constitute a very important group of spectrum of action (α-, β-, or dopamine-receptor affinity) or drugs used for cardiovascular, respiratory, and other conditions. mode of action (direct or indirect). They are readily divided into subgroups on the basis of their Sympathomimetic agonists Direct-acting Indirect-acting Alpha agonists Beta agonists Releasers Reuptake inhibitors (amphetamine) (cocaine) Beta2-selective (albuterol) Beta1-selective (dobutamine) Nonselective (isoproterenol) Alpha2-selective (clonidine) Alpha1-selective (phenylephrine) Nonselective (norepinephrine) CLASSIFICATION and, when given as a drug, can also activate β receptors (interme- diate doses) and α receptors (larger doses).","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spectrum of Action"},{"label":"B","text":"Mode of ActionAdrenoceptors are classified as α, β, or dopamine receptors; these groups are further subdivided into subgroups. The distribution Sympathomimetic agonists may directly activate their adreno- of these receptors is set forth in Table 9–1. Epinephrine may be ceptors, or they may act indirectly to increase the concentra- considered a single prototype agonist with effects at all α- and tion of endogenous catecholamine transmitter in the synapse. β-receptor types. Alternatively, separate prototypes, phenylephrine Amphetamine derivatives and tyramine cause the release of (an α agonist) and isoproterenol (β) may be defined. The just- stored catecholamines; they are therefore mainly indirect in their mentioned drugs have relatively little effect on dopamine recep- mode of action. Cocaine and the tricyclic antidepressants exhibit tors, but dopamine itself is a potent dopamine receptor agonist another form of indirect action; these drugs inhibit reuptake of 79 80 PART II Autonomic Drugs High-Yield Terms to Learn Anorexiant A drug that decreases appetite (causes anorexia) Catecholamine A dihydroxyphenylethylamine derivative (eg, norepinephrine, epinephrine), a relatively polar molecule that is readily metabolized by catechol-O-methyltransferase Decongestant An α-agonist drug that reduces conjunctival, nasal, or oropharyngeal mucosal vasodilation by constricting blood vessels in the submucosal tissue Mydriatic A drug that causes dilation of the pupil; opposite of miotic Phenylisopropylamine A derivative of phenylisopropylamine (eg, amphetamine, ephedrine). Unlike catecholamines, phenylisopropylamines usually have oral activity, a long half-life, CNS activity, and cause release of stored catecholamines Selective ` agonist, a agonist Drugs that have relatively greater effects on α or β adrenoceptors; none are absolutely selective or specific Sympathomimetic A drug that mimics stimulation of the sympathetic autonomic nervous system Reuptake inhibitor An indirect-acting drug that increases the activity of transmitters in the synapse by inhibiting their reuptake into the presynaptic nerve ending. May act selectively on noradrenergic, serotonergic, or both types of nerve endings TABLE 9–1 Types of adrenoceptors, some of the peripheral tissues in which they are found, and their major effects. Type Tissue Actions α1 Most vascular smooth muscle Contracts (↑ vascular resistance) Pupillary dilator muscle Contracts (mydriasis) Pilomotor smooth muscle Contracts (erects hair) Liver (in some species, eg, rat) Stimulates glycogenolysis α2 Adrenergic and cholinergic nerve terminals Inhibits transmitter release Platelets Stimulates aggregation Some vascular smooth muscle Contracts Fat cells Inhibits lipolysis Pancreatic β (B) cells Inhibits insulin release β1 Heart Stimulates rate and force Juxtaglomerular cells of kidney Stimulates renin release β2 Airways, uterine, and vascular smooth muscle Relaxes Liver (human) Stimulates glycogenolysis Pancreatic β (B) cells Stimulates insulin release Somatic motor neuron terminals (voluntary muscle) Causes tremor Heart Stimulates rate and force β3 Fat cells Stimulates lipolysis Dopamine1 (D1) Renal and other splanchnic blood vessels Dilates (↓ resistance) Dopamine2 (D2) Nerve terminals Inhibits adenylyl cyclase CHAPTER 9 Sympathomimetics 81 catecholamines by the norepinephrine transporter (NET) and the C. Dopamine Receptor Effects dopamine transporter (DAT) in nerve terminals (see Figure 6–2) Dopamine D1 receptors activate adenylyl cyclase via Gs andand thus increase the synaptic activity of released transmitter. increase cAMP in neurons and vascular smooth muscle. Dopamine Blockade of metabolism (ie, block of catechol-O-methyltransferase D2 receptors are more important in the brain but probably also[COMT] and monoamine oxidase [MAO]) has little direct effect play a significant role as presynaptic receptors on peripheral on autonomic activity, but MAO inhibition increases the stores nerves. These receptors reduce the synthesis of cAMP via Gi.of catecholamines and related molecules in adrenergic synaptic vesicles and thus may potentiate the action of indirect-acting sym- pathomimetics that cause the release of stored transmitter. ORGAN SYSTEM EFFECTS"},{"label":"A","text":"Central Nervous System CHEMISTRY & PHARMACOKINETICS Catecholamines do not enter the CNS effectively. Sympatho- mimetics that do enter the CNS (eg, amphetamines) have a The endogenous adrenoceptor agonists (epinephrine, norepi- spectrum of stimulant effects, beginning with mild alerting or nephrine, and dopamine) are catecholamines and are rapidly reduction of fatigue and progressing to anorexia, euphoria, and metabolized by COMT and MAO. If used as drugs, these insomnia. Some of these central effects probably reflect the release adrenoceptor agonists are inactive by the oral route and must of dopamine in certain dopaminergic tracts. Repeated dosing of be given parenterally. When released from nerve endings, they amphetamines results in the rapid development of tolerance and are subsequently taken up (by NET or DAT) into nerve end- dependence. These CNS effects reflect the amplification of dopa- ings and into perisynaptic cells; this uptake may also occur with mine’s action in the ventral tegmental area and other CNS nuclei norepinephrine, epinephrine, and dopamine given as drugs. These (see Chapter 32). Very high doses of amphetamines lead to marked agonists have a short duration of action. When given parenterally, anxiety or aggressiveness, paranoia, and, less commonly, seizures. they do not enter the central nervous system (CNS) in significant Overdoses of cocaine very commonly result in seizures. amounts. Isoproterenol, a synthetic cat