iability. Hypertension, dopamine reuptake (DAT) action arrhythmias, seizures Tyramine Tyramine Displaces stored No clinical use but Normally high first-pass Hypertension, arrhythmias, catecholamines found in fermented effect, but in patients stroke, myocardial foods taking MAO inhibitors infarction it is absorbed ADHD, attention-deficit hyperactivity disorder; CNS, central nervous system; DAT, dopamine transporter; MAO, monoamine oxidase. This page intentionally left blank C H A P T E R Adrenoceptor Blockers 10 Alpha- and beta-adrenoceptor-blocking agents are divided into Because α and β blockers differ markedly in their effects and primary subgroups on the basis of their receptor selectivity. All clinical applications, these drugs are considered separately in of these agents are pharmacologic antagonists or partial agonists. the following discussion. Adrenoceptor antagonists Alpha blockers Beta blockers Alpha2-selective Beta2-selective (yohimbine) (butoxamine) Alpha1-selective Beta1-selective (prazosin) (atenolol) Nonselective Nonselective (propranolol) Reversible Irreversible (phentolamine) (phenoxybenzamine) ALPHA-BLOCKING DRUGS selectivity is discussed in the following text. Alpha2-selective— Yohimbine and rauwolscine are α2-selective competitive phar","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Classification macologic antagonists. They are used primarily in research applications. Subdivisions of the α blockers are based on selective affinity for α1 versus α2 receptors or a lack thereof. Other features used to classify B. Pharmacokineticsthe α-blocking drugs are their reversibility and duration of action. Irreversible, long-acting—Phenoxybenzamine is the pro- Alpha-blocking drugs are all active by the oral as well as the totypical long-acting, irreversible α blocker. It is only slightly parenteral route, although phentolamine is rarely given orally. α1-selective. Reversible, shorter-acting—Phentolamine is a Phenoxybenzamine has a short elimination half-life but a long competitive, reversible blocking agent that does not distinguish duration of action—about 48 h—because it binds covalently to between α1 and α2 receptors. Alpha1-selective—Prazosin is a its receptor. Phentolamine has a duration of action of 2–4 h when highly selective, reversible pharmacologic α1 blocker. Doxazosin, used orally and 20–40 min when given parenterally. Prazosin and terazosin, and tamsulosin are similar drugs. The advantage of α1 the other α1-selective blockers act for 8–24 h. 89 90 PART II Autonomic Drugs High-Yield Terms to Learn Competitive blocker A surmountable antagonist (eg, phentolamine); one that can be overcome by increasing the dose of agonist Epinephrine reversal Conversion of the pressor response to epinephrine (typical of large doses) to a blood pressure–lowering effect; caused by α blockers, which unmask the β2 effects of epinephrine Intrinsic sympathomimetic Partial agonist action by adrenoceptor blockers; typical of several β blockers (eg, pindolol, activity (ISA) acebutolol) Irreversible blocker A nonsurmountable inhibitor, usually because of covalent bond formation (eg, phenoxybenzamine) Membrane-stabilizing Local anesthetic action; typical of several β blockers (eg, propranolol) activity (MSA) Orthostatic hypotension Hypotension that is most marked in the upright position; caused by venous pooling (typical of α blockade) or inadequate blood volume (caused by blood loss or excessive diuresis) Partial agonist A drug (eg, pindolol) that produces a smaller maximal effect than a full agonist and therefore can inhibit the effect of a full agonist Pheochromocytoma A tumor consisting of cells that release varying amounts of norepinephrine and epinephrine into the circulation"},{"label":"C","text":"Mechanism of Action 2. Selective α blockers—Because prazosin and its analogs block vascular α1 receptors much more effectively than thePhenoxybenzamine binds covalently to the α receptor, thereby α2-modulatory receptors associated with cardiac sympatheticproducing an irreversible (insurmountable) blockade. The other nerve endings, these drugs cause much less reflex tachycardia than agents are competitive antagonists, and their effects can be sur- the nonselective α blockers when reducing blood pressure. These mounted by increased concentrations of agonist. This difference drugs also have useful effects on smooth muscle in the prostate. may be important in the treatment of pheochromocytoma because a massive release of catecholamines from the tumor may overcome a reversible blockade. E. Clinical Uses"}],"priority":2,"status":"quarantine","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"quarantine","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","long_stem_manual_review","mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-855d3eb1b028","stem":"Local anesthetic activity—Local anesthetic activity BETA-BLOCKING DRUGS (“membrane-stabilizing activity”) is a disadvantage when β block- ers are used topically in the eye because it decreases protective","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Anesthesiology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Classification, Subgroups, reflexes and increases the risk of corneal ulceration. Local anes- thetic effects are absent from timolol and several other β blockers and Mechanisms that are useful in glaucoma. All of the β blockers 