he new drug most closely (B) Edrophonium resemble?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-47c7c3ab5d58","stem":"Phenylephrine, an α agonist, induces bradycardia through the baroreceptor reflex. Blockade of this drug’s α-mediated vaso- Concentration of albuterol constrictor effect prevents the bradycardia. The answer is A. 96 PART II Autonomic Drugs C H E C K L I S T When you complete this chapter, you should be able to: ❑ Describe and compare the effects of an α blocker on the blood pressure and heart rate responses to epinephrine, norepinephrine, and phenylephrine. ❑ Compare the pharmacodynamics of propranolol, labetalol, metoprolol, and pindolol. ❑ Compare the pharmacokinetics of propranolol, atenolol, esmolol, and nadolol. ❑ Describe the clinical indications and toxicities of typical α and β blockers. CHAPTER 10 Adrenoceptor Blockers 97 DRUG SUMMARY TABLE: Adrenoceptor Blockers Mechanism Clinical Toxicities, Subclass of Action Applications Pharmacokinetics Interactions Nonselective ` blockers Phentolamine Competitive pharmacologic Pheochromocytoma, Oral, IV • short half-life Orthostatic hypotension • reflex antagonism at α receptors antidote to overdose Duration: 2–4 h tachycardia of α agonists Phenoxybenzamine Irreversible (covalent) binding Pheochromocytoma, Oral, short half-life but Orthostatic hypotension, reflex to α receptors carcinoid, mastocyto- long duration of action tachycardia • gastrointestinal sis, Raynaud’s (24–48 h) irritation phenomenon `1-Selective blockers Prazosin Competitive antagonism at Hypertension, benign Oral Orthostatic hypotension α1 receptors prostatic hyperplasia Duration: 8 h (especially first dose), but little reflex tachycardia Doxazosin, terazosin: like prazosin; longer duration of action (12–24 h) Tamsulosin, silodosin: like prazosin, approved only for benign prostatic hyperplasia `2-Selective blockers Yohimbine Competitive antagonism at Obsolete use for Oral, parenteral Tachycardia • gastrointestinal α2 receptors erectile dysfunction • upset research use Nonselective a blockers Propranolol Competitive block of Angina, arrhythmias Oral and IV Excessive β blockade: β receptors, local anesthetic (treatment and pro- Duration: 4–6 h. Ready bronchospasm (can be fatal in effect phylaxis), hyperten- entry into CNS asthmatics), atrioventricular sion, thyrotoxicosis, block, heart failure • CNS tremor, stage fright, sedation, lethargy, sleep migraine disturbances Timolol, betaxolol: lack local anesthetic action; useful in glaucoma Pindolol: partial agonist action; possibly safer in asthma Nadolol: like propranolol but longer action (up to 24 h) and less CNS effect a1-Selective blockers Atenolol Competitive block of Hypertension, angina, Oral Like propranolol with β1 receptors arrhythmias Duration: 6–9 h somewhat less danger of bronchospasm Esmolol: IV agent for perioperative and thyroid storm arrhythmias, hypertensive emergency Metoprolol: like atenolol, oral, shown to reduce mortality in heart failure Nebivolol: new oral β1-selective blocker with additional vasodilating action a2-Selective blockers Butoxamine Competitive block of None • research use — Bronchospasm β2 receptors only ` + a blockers Labetalol Four isomers; 2 bind and block Hypertension, Oral and IV Like atenolol both α and β receptors hypertensive Duration: 5 h emergencies (IV) Carvedilol: like labetalol, 2 isomers; shown to reduce mortality in heart failure This page intentionally left blank PART III CARDIOVASCULAR DRUGS C H A P T E R Drugs Used in Hypertension 11 Antihypertensive drugs are organized around a clinical vasodilation, and antagonism of angiotensin, and many agents indication—the need to treat a disease—rather than a recep- are available in most categories. A single renin inhibitor has tor type. The drugs covered in this unit have a variety of recently been added to the drugs used in this condition. mechanisms of action including diuresis, sympathoplegia, Drugs used in hypertension Diuretics Sympathoplegics— VasodilatV ors Angiotensin Renin blockers of antagonists inhibitor (aliskiren) Alpha or beta ACE Receptor receptors inhibitors blockers (prazosin, (captopril) (losartan) propranolol) Nerve terminals Parenteral (guanethidine) vasodilators Calcium (nitroprusside) blockers Older oral Ganglia (nifedipine) vasodilators (hexamethonium) (hydralazine) CNS sympathetic outflow (clonidine) 99 100 PART III Cardiovascular Drugs High-Yield Terms to Learn Baroreceptor reflex Primary autonomic mechanism for blood pressure homeostasis; involves sensory input from carotid sinus and aorta to the vasomotor center and output via the parasympathetic and sympathetic motor nerves Catecholamine reuptake Nerve terminal transporter responsible for recycling norepinephrine after release into the pump (norepinephrine synapse transporter [NET]) Catecholamine vesicle Storage vesicle transporter that pumps amine from cytoplasm into vesicle; also called pump vesicle monoamine transporter (VMAT) End-organ damage Vascular damage in heart, kidney, retina, or brain Essential hypertension Hypertension of unknown etiology; also called primary hypertension False transmitter Substance, for example, octopamine, stored in vesicles and released into synaptic cleft but lacking the effect of the true transmitter, for example, norepinephrine Hypertensive emergency An accelerated form of severe hypertension associated with rising blood pressure and (“malignant rapidly progressing damage to vessels and end organs. Often signaled by renal damage, hypertension”) encephalopathy, and retinal hemorrhages or by angina, stroke, or myocardial infarction Orthostatic hypotension Hypotension on assuming upright posture; postural hypotension Postganglionic neuron Drug that blocks transmission by an action in the terminals of the postganglionic nerves blocker Rebound hypertension Elevated blood pressure (usually above pretreatment levels) resulting from loss of antihypertensive drug effect Reflex tachycardia Tachycardia resulting from lowering of blood pressure; mediated by the baroreceptor reflex Stepped care Progressive addition of drugs to a regimen, starting with one (usually a diuretic) and adding in stepwise fashion a sympatholytic, an ACE inhibitor, and (sometimes) a vasodilator Sympatholytic, Drug that reduces effects of the sympathetic nervous system sympathoplegic Less than 20% of cases of hypertension are due to (“secondary” drugs lower blood pressure by reduction of blood volume and to) factors that can be clearly defined and corrected. This type probably also by a direct vascular effect that is not fully under- of hypertension is associated with pheochromocytoma, coarcta- stood. The diuretics most important for treating hypertension are tion of the aorta, renal vascular disease, adrenal cortical tumors, the thiazides (eg, hydrochlorothiazide) and the loop diuretics and a few other rare conditions. Most cases of hypertension are (eg, furosemide). Thiazides may be adequate in mild hyperten- idiopathic, also called “primary” or “essential” hypertension. The sion, but the loop agents are used in moderate and severe hyper- strategies for treating idiopathic hypertension are based on the tension and in hypertensive emergencies. Compensatory responses determinants of arterial pressure (see Figure 6–4). These strategies to blood pressure lowering by diuretics are minimal (Table 11–1). include reductions of blood volume, sympathetic effects, vascular When thiazides are given