e in (β blockers reduce renin release from the kidney). Nebivolol is hypertension of any severity. Verapamil and diltiazem also reduce a newer β blocker with some direct vasodilator action. Potential cardiac output in most patients. Nifedipine is the prototype dihy- adverse effects are listed in the Drug Summary Table. As noted dropyridine calcium channel blocker, and many other dihydro- in Chapter 10, β1-selective blockers with fewer CNS effects may pyridines are available (eg, amlodipine, felodipine, isradipine). have some advantages over the nonselective and more lipid- Because they produce fewer compensatory responses, the calcium soluble agents. channel blockers are much more commonly used than hydralazine or minoxidil. They are discussed in greater detail in Chapter 12. VASODILATORS C. Nitroprusside, Diazoxide, Drugs that dilate blood vessels by acting directly on smooth and Fenoldopam muscle cells through nonautonomic mechanisms are useful in These parenteral vasodilators are used in hypertensive emergencies. treating some hypertensive patients. Vasodilators act by four Nitroprusside is a light-sensitive, short-acting agent (duration of major mechanisms: release of nitric oxide, opening of potassium action is a few minutes) that must be infused continuously. The channels (which leads to hyperpolarization), blockade of calcium release of nitric oxide (from the drug molecule itself) stimulates channels, and activation of D1 dopamine receptors (Table 11–2). guanylyl cyclase and increases cyclic guanine monophosphate Compensatory responses are listed in Table 11–1. (cGMP) concentration in smooth muscle. Diazoxide is a thiazide derivative but lacks diuretic proper-"},{"label":"A","text":"Hydralazine and Minoxidil ties. It is given as intravenous boluses or as an infusion and has duration of action of several hours. Diazoxide opens potassiumThese older vasodilators have more effect on arterioles than on channels, thus hyperpolarizing and relaxing smooth muscle cells.veins. They are orally active and suitable for chronic therapy. This drug also reduces insulin release and can be used to treatHydralazine apparently acts through the release of nitric oxide hypoglycemia caused by insulin-producing tumors.from endothelial cells. However, it is rarely used at high dosage Dopamine D1 receptor activation by fenoldopam causesbecause of its toxicity (Drug Summary Table). Hydralazine- prompt, marked arteriolar vasodilation. This drug is given byinduced lupus erythematosus is reversible upon stopping the drug, intravenous infusion. It has a short duration of action (10 min)and lupus is uncommon at dosages below 200 mg/d. and is used for hypertensive emergencies. Minoxidil is extremely efficacious, and systemic administra- tion is reserved for severe hypertension. Minoxidil is a prodrug; its metabolite, minoxidil sulfate, is a potassium channel opener ANGIOTENSIN ANTAGONISTS & that hyperpolarizes and relaxes vascular smooth muscle. The A RENIN INHIBITORcompensatory responses to minoxidil (Figure 11–1) require the concomitant use of diuretics and β blockers. Because it can cause The two primary groups of angiotensin antagonists are the hirsutism, minoxidil is also available as a topical agent for the angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors and the treatment of baldness. angiotensin II receptor blockers (ARBs). ACE inhibitors (eg, captopril), which inhibit the enzyme variously known as angiotensin-"},{"label":"B","text":"Calcium Channel-Blocking Agents converting enzyme, kininase II, and peptidyl dipeptidase, cause a reduction in blood levels of angiotensin II and aldosterone and an Calcium channel blockers (eg, nifedipine, verapamil, diltiazem) increase in endogenous vasodilators of the kinin family (bradykinin; are effective vasodilators; because they are moderately efficacious Figure 11–3). ACE inhibitors have a low incidence of serious adverse effects when given in normal dosage (except in pregnancy) and produce minimal compensatory responses (Table 11–1). The TABLE 11–2 Mechanisms of action of vasodilators. ACE inhibitors are useful in heart failure and diabetes as well as in hypertension. The toxicities of ACE inhibitors include cough (up Mechanism of Smooth Muscle to 30% of patients) and renal damage in occasional patients with Relaxation Examples preexisting renal vascular disease and in the fetus (although they Release of nitric oxide from drug or Nitroprusside, hydralazine protect the diabetic kidney). These drugs are absolutely contraindi- endothelium cated in pregnancy. The second group of angiotensin antagonists, Hyperpolarization of vascular smooth Minoxidil sulfate, diazoxide the receptor blockers, is represented by the orally active agents muscle through opening of potassium losartan, valsartan, irbesartan, candesartan, and other ARBs, channels which competitively inhibit angiotensin II at its AT1 receptor site. Reduction of calcium influx via L-type Verapamil, diltiazem, ARBs appear to be as effective in lowering blood pressure as the channels nifedipine ACE inhibitors and have the advantage of a lower incidence of cough. However, like the ACE inhibitors, they do cause fetal renal Activation of dopamine D1 receptors Fenoldopam toxicity and are thus contraindicated in pregnancy. 104 PART III Cardiovascular Drugs However, overall toxicity can be reduced and compensatory Angiotensinogen responses minimized by the use of multiple drugs at lower dos- ages in patients with moderate or severe hypertension. Typically, Renin − Aliskiren drugs are added to a patient’s regimen in stepwise fashion; each additional agent is chosen from a different subgroup until Angiotensin I Bradykinin adequate blood pressure control has been achieved. The usual steps (inactive decapeptide) (active vasodilator) include (1) lifestyle measures such as salt restriction and weight reduc- tion, (2) diuretics (a thiazide), (3) sympathoplegics (a β blocker), Angiotensin- (4) ACE inhibitors, and (5) vasodilators. The vasodilator chosen converting − ACE inhibitors enzyme first is usually a calcium channel blocker. The ability of drugs in steps 2 and 3 to control the compensatory responses induced by Angiotensin II Inactive metabolites the others should be noted (eg, propranolol reduces the tachy- (active vasoconstrictor) cardia induced by hydralazine). Thus, rational polypharmacy minimizes toxicities while producing additive or supra-additive therapeutic effects. − AT1 receptor blockers AT1 receptor SKILL KEEPER 2: COMPENSATORY RESPONSES TO ANTIHYPERTENSIVE DRUGS FIGURE 11–3 Actions of aliskiren, angiotensin-converting (SEE CHAPTER 6) enzyme inhibitors, and AT1 receptor blockers. Renin converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I. Block by aliskiren blocks the If hydralazine is administered in moderate dosage for sequence at its start. ACE is responsible for activating angiotensin I to several weeks, compensatory cardiac and renal responses angiotensin II and for inactivating bradykinin, a vasodilator will be observed. Specify the exact mechanisms and struc- normally present in very low concentrations. Block of this enzyme tures involved in these responses. The Skill Keeper Answer thus decreases the concentration of a vasoconstrictor and increases appears at the end of the chapter. the concentration of a vasodilator. The AT1 receptor antagonists lack the effect on bradykinin levels, which may explain the lower incidence of cough observed with these agents."},{"label":"B","text":"Monotherapy It has been found in large clinical studies that many patients do well on a single drug (eg, an ACE inhibitor, calcium channel The newest drug in the antihypertensive group is aliskiren, blocker, or combined α and β blocker). This approach to the an inhibitor of renin’s action on its substrate, angiotensinogen. It treatment of mild and moderate hypertension has become more thus reduces the formation of angiotensin I and, in consequence, popular than stepped care because of its simplicity, better patient angiotensin II. Toxicities include headache and diarrhea. It does compliance, and—with modern drugs—a relatively low incidence not appear to cause cough, but it is not yet known whether it of toxicity. has the other toxicities of the angiotensin antagonists. It does not show reproductive toxicity in animals but is considered to"},{"label":"C","text":"Age and Ethnicitybe contraindicated in pregnancy because of the toxicity of ACE inhibitors and ARBs. Older patients of most races respond better to diuretics and β block- Angiotensin antagonists and renin inhibitors reduce aldoste- ers than to ACE inhibitors. African Americans of all ages respond rone levels (angiotensin II is a major stimulant of aldosterone better to diuretics and calcium channel blockers, and they respond release) and cause potassium retention. If the patient has renal less well to ACE inhibitors. There is considerable interindividual impairment, is consuming a high-potassium diet, or is taking variability in metabolism of β blockers. other drugs that tend to conserve potassium, potassium concen- trations may reach toxic levels. D. Hypertensive Emergency Hypertensive emergency (formerly called malignant hypertension) CLINICAL USES OF ANTIHYPERTENSIVE is an accelerated form of severe hypertension associated with ris- ing blood pressure and rapidly progressing damage to vessels and DRUGS end organs. Management of hypertensive emergency must be carried out on an urgent basis in the hospital. Powerful vasodila-"},{"label":"A","text":"Stepped Care (Polypharmacy) tors (nitroprusside, fenoldopam, or diazoxide) are combined with Therapy of hypertension is complex because the disease is diuretics (furosemide) and β blockers to lower blood pressure symptomless until far advanced and because the drugs may to the 140–160/90–110 mm Hg range promptly (within a few cause major compensatory responses and significant toxicities. hours). Further reduction is then pursued more slowly. CHAPTER 11 Drugs Used in Hypertension 105 QUESTIONS 8. A patient with hypertension is to receive a calcium channel"}],"priority":2,"status":"quarantine","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","too_many_options_possible_merge","duplicate_option_labels","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"quarantine","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","long_stem_manual_review","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions","requires_visual_review","too_many_options","too_many_options_possible_merge","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-6e2081704276","stem":"A 32-year-old woman with hypertension wishes to become (A) Diarrhea pregnant. Her physician informs her that she will have to (B) Hypoglycemia switch to another antihypertensive drug. Which of the follow- (C) Increased PR interval ing drugs is absolutely contraindicated in pregnancy?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Drugs","subtopic":"Antihypertensive","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1f6bf810cd63","stem":"A significant number of patients started on ACE inhibitor because it has a good safety record. Alpha (choice D) and therapy for hypertension are intolerant and must be switched β blockers (choices A and E) are not contraindicated. In con- to