 beats (PVBs). digitoxin, which also comes from the foxglove, is no longer avail- When PVBs are coupled to normal beats in a 1:1 fashion, the able in the United States. Digoxin has an oral bioavailability of rhythm is called bigeminy (Figure 13–4). 60–75%, and a half-life of 36–40 h. Elimination is by renal excre- tion (about 60%) and hepatic metabolism (40%)."},{"label":"D","text":"Clinical Uses"},{"label":"B","text":"Mechanism of Action 1. Congestive heart failure—Digitalis is the traditional Inhibition of Na+/K+ ATPase of the cell membrane by digitalis is positive inotropic agent used in the treatment of chronic heart well documented and is considered to be the primary biochemical failure. However, careful clinical studies indicate that while digi- mechanism of action (Figure 13–3). Inhibition of Na+/K+ ATPase talis may improve functional status (reducing symptoms), it does results in a small increase in intracellular sodium. The increased not prolong life. Other agents (diuretics, ACE inhibitors, vasodi- sodium alters the driving force for sodium-calcium exchange by lators) may be equally effective and less toxic, and some of these the exchanger, NCX, so that less calcium is removed from the cell. alternative therapies do prolong life (see later discussion). Because The increased intracellular calcium is stored in the sarcoplasmic the half-lives of cardiac glycosides are long, the drugs accumulate reticulum and upon release increases contractile force. Other significantly in the body, and dosing regimens must be carefully mechanisms of action for digitalis have been proposed, but they designed and monitored. 124 PART III Cardiovascular Drugs Digoxin – Interstitium Cell membrane Na+/K+ NCX Cav–L Cytoplasm ATP – Ca2+channel blockers Na+ K+ Ca2+ Trigger Ca2+ SERCA ATP CalS CalS Sarcoplasmic Ca2+ Ca2+ Ca2+ reticulum CalS CalS CalS ATP RyR Ca2+sensitizers Ca2+ Ca2+ + Actin-tropomyosin-troponin Myosin Z line Sarcomere FIGURE 13–3 Schematic diagram of a cardiac sarcomere with the cellular components involved in excitation-contraction coupling and the sites of action of several drugs. Factors involved in excitation-contraction coupling include Na+/K+ ATPase; Na+/Ca2+ exchanger, NCX; voltage-gated calcium channel (Cav-L); calcium transporter (SERCA) in the wall of the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR); calcium release channel in the SR, RyR (ryanodine receptor); and the site of calcium interaction with the troponin-tropomyosin system. CalS, calsequestrin, a calcium-binding protein in the SR. (Modified and reproduced, with permission, from Katzung BG, editor: Basic & Clinical Pharmacology, 11th ed. McGraw-Hill, 2009: Fig. 13–1.)"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-d44266c2e8dd","stem":"Correction of potassium or magnesium deficiency— Digoxin has a narrow therapeutic window, and its dosing Correction of potassium deficiency (caused, eg, by diuretic use) is must be carefully managed. The drug’s minimum effective useful in chronic digitalis intoxication. Mild toxicity may often be concentration is about 1 ng/mL. About 60% is excreted in the managed by omitting 1 or 2 doses of digitalis and giving oral or urine; the rest is metabolized in the liver. The normal parenteral K+ supplements. Severe acute intoxication (as in suicidal clearance of digoxin is 7 L/h/70 kg; volume of distribution is 500 L/70 kg; and bioavailability is 70%. If your 70-kg patient’s renal function is only 30% of normal, what daily oral NSR PVB NSR PVB maintenance dosage should be used to achieve a safe plasma concentration of 1 ng/mL? The Skill Keeper Answer appears at the end of the chapter. V6 OTHER DRUGS USED IN CONGESTIVE ST HEART FAILURE FIGURE 13–4 Electrocardiographic record showing digitalis- induced bigeminy. The complexes marked NSR are normal sinus The other major agents used in heart failure include diuretics, rhythm beats; an inverted T wave and depressed ST segment are ACE inhibitors, β1-selective sympathomimetics, β blockers, present. The complexes marked PVB are premature ventricular beats. phosphodiesterase inhibitors, and vasodilators. 126 PART III Cardiovascular Drugs","type":"ECG Interpretation","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Drugs","subtopic":"Diuretic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diuretics F. Vasodilators Diuretics are the first-line therapy for both systolic and diastolic Vasodilator therapy with nitroprusside or nitroglycerin is often failure and are used in heart failure before digitalis and other drugs used for acute severe failure with congestion. The use of these are considered. Furosemide is a very useful agent for immediate vasodilator drugs is based on the reduction in cardiac size and reduction of the pulmonary congestion and severe edema associ- improved efficiency that can be achieved with proper adjustment ated with acute heart failure and for moderate or severe chronic of venous return (preload) and reduction of impedance to ven- failure. Thiazides such as hydrochlorothiazide are sometimes tricular ejection (afterload). Vasodilator therapy can be dramati- sufficient for mild chronic failure. Clinical studies suggest that cally effective, especially in cases in which increased afterload is a spironolactone and eplerenone (aldosterone antagonist diuretics) major factor in causing the failure (eg, continuing hypertension have significant long-term benefits and can reduce mortality in in an individual who has just had an infarct). The natriuretic chronic failure. Diuretics are discussed in Chapter 15. peptide nesiritide acts chiefly by causing vasodilation, although it does have natriuretic effects as well. It is given by IV infusion"},{"label":"B","text":"Angiotensin Antagonists for acute failure only. Nesiritide has significant renal toxicity and renal function must be monitored. Chronic heart failure some- These agents have been shown to reduce morbidity and mortal- times responds favorably to oral vasodilators such as hydralazine ity in chronic heart failure. Although they have no direct positive or isosorbide dinitrate (or both), and this combination has been inotropic action, angiotensin antagonists reduce aldosterone shown to reduce mortality in African Americans. Calcium channel secretion, salt and water retention, and vascular resistance (see blockers (eg, verapamil) are of no value in heart failure. Chapter 11). They are now considered, along with diuretics, to be first-line drugs for chronic heart failure. The angiotensin receptor"},{"label":"G","text":"Nonpharmacologic Therapyblockers (ARBs, eg, losartan) appear to have the same benefits as ACE inhibitors (eg, captopril), although experience with ARBs is A variety of surgical procedures to remove nonfunctional regions not as extensive. of damaged myocardium have been attempted with mixed results. Resynchronization of right and left ventricular contraction by"},{"label":"C","text":"Beta1-Adrenoceptor Agonists means of a pacemaker has been beneficial in patients with long QRS (indicating conduction abnormalities). Patients with coro- Dobutamine and dopamine are often useful in acute failure in nary artery disease and heart failure may have improved systolic which systolic function is markedly depressed (see Chapter 9). function after coronary revascularization. However, they are not appropriate for chronic failure because of tolerance, lack of oral efficacy, and significant arrhythmogenic effects. QUESTIONS Questions 1–2. A 73-year-old man with an inadequate responseD. Beta-Adrenoceptor Antagonists to other drugs is to receive digoxin for heart failure. Several β blockers (carvedilol, labetalol, metoprolol, Chapter 10)"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-e2b1ae735e45","stem":"A 65-year-old woman has been admitted to the coronary (G) Enalapril care unit with a left ventricular myocardial infarction. She (H) Furosemide develops acute severe heart failure with marked pulmonary (I) Metoprolol edema, but no evidence of peripheral edema or weight gain. (J) Spironolactone Which one of the following drugs would be most useful? (A) Digoxin 9. Which of the following has been shown to prolong life in (B) Furosemide patients with chronic congestive failure in spite of having a (C) Minoxidil negative inotropic effect on cardiac contractility? (D) Propranolol (A) Carvedilol (E) Spironolactone (B) Digoxin (C) Dobutamine","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["possible_multiple_questions_in_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a01124443a74","stem":"An 82-year-old woman has long-standing heart failure. (D) Enalapril Which one of the following drugs has been shown to reduce (E) Furosemide mortality in chronic heart failure? (A) Atenolol 10. A 5-year-old child is brought to the emergency department (B) Digoxin with sinus arrest and a ventricular rate of 35 bpm. An empty (C) Dobutamine bottle of his uncle’s digoxin was found where he was playing. (D) Furosemide Which of the following is the drug of choice in treating a (E) Spironolactone severe overdose of digoxin? (A) Digoxin antibodies","type":"Short Answer","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5f20052ac4de","stem":"Which row in the following table correctly shows the major (B) Lidocaine infusion effects of full therapeutic doses of digoxin on the AV node (C) Magnesium infusion and the ECG?","type":"ECG Interpretation","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","options":[],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e78103c18852","stem":"Which one of the following drugs is associated with clinically cium, including calcium overload, the most important cause useful or physiologically im