owing statements is most correct? (E) Verapamil which of the following should be given immediately? (C) Nitroprusside channels? (D) Esmolol drugs would be most suitable?","type":"ECG Interpretation","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Drugs","subtopic":"Antiarrhythmic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"B","text":"Pharmacokinetics, Clinical drugs also cause local anesthetic-like CNS toxicity. Hyperkalemia Uses, and Toxicities increases the cardiac toxicity of these agents. Pharmacokinetics of the major drugs are listed in the Drug Summary Table at the end of the chapter. GROUP 2 ANTIARRHYTHMICS (BETA BLOCKERS)1. Group 1A drugs—Procainamide can be used in all types of arrhythmias: atrial and ventricular arrhythmias are most responsive."},{"label":"A","text":"Prototypes, Mechanisms, and EffectsQuinidine and disopyramide have similar effects but are used much less frequently. Procainamide is also commonly used in Beta blockers are discussed in more detail in Chapter 10. arrhythmias during the acute phase of myocardial infarction. Propranolol and esmolol are prototypic antiarrhythmic β Procainamide may cause hypotension and a reversible syn- blockers. Their mechanism in arrhythmias is primarily cardiac drome similar to lupus erythematosus. Quinidine causes cinchonism β-adrenoceptor blockade and reduction in cAMP, which results (headache, vertigo, tinnitus); cardiac depression; gastrointesti- in the reduction of both sodium and calcium currents and the nal upset; and autoimmune reactions (eg, thrombocytopenic suppression of abnormal pacemakers. The AV node is particularly 136 PART III Cardiovascular Drugs sensitive to β blockers and the PR interval is usually prolonged by group 2 drugs (Table 14–1). Under some conditions, these drugs Group 3 action may have some direct local anesthetic (sodium channel-blocking) 0 mV effect in the heart, but this is probably rare at the concentrations achieved clinically. Sotalol and amiodarone, generally classified as group 3 drugs, also have group 2 β-blocking effects. Phase 3 (IK)"},{"label":"B","text":"Clinical Uses and Toxicities ERP −85 mV Esmolol, a very short-acting β blocker for intravenous administra- Na Ca Group 3 actiontion, is used exclusively in acute arrhythmias. Propranolol, meto- Outside prolol, and timolol are commonly used as prophylactic drugs in patients who have had a myocardial infarction. Membrane The toxicities of β blockers are the same in patients with arrhythmias as in patients with other conditions (Chapter 10 and Inside K K1 Na Ca Drug Summary Table). While patients with arrhythmias are often Action potential currents Diastolic currents more prone to β-blocker-induced depression of cardiac output than are patients with normal hearts, it should be noted that judicious use of these drugs reduces progression of chronic heart FIGURE 14–5 Schematic diagram of the effects of group 3 failure (Chapter 13) and reduces the incidence of potentially fatal agents. All group 3 drugs prolong the AP duration in susceptible arrhythmias in this condition. cardiac cells by reducing the outward (repolarizing) phase 3 potassium current (IK, wavy lines). The main effect is to prolong the effective refractory period (ERP). Note that the phase 4 diastolic SKILL KEEPER: CHARACTERISTICS OF potassium current (IK1) is not affected by these drugs. a BLOCKERS (SEE CHAPTER 10) Describe the important subgroups of β blockers and their major pharmacokinetic and pharmacodynamic features. C. Amiodarone: A Special Case The Skill Keeper Answer appears at the end of the chapter. Amiodarone is useful in most types of arrhythmias and is consid- ered the most efficacious of all antiarrhythmic drugs. This may be because it has a broad spectrum: It blocks sodium, calcium, and potassium channels and β adrenoceptors. Because of its toxicities,GROUP 3 ANTIARRHYTHMICS however, amiodarone is approved for use mainly in arrhythmias (POTASSIUM IK CHANNEL BLOCKERS) that are resistant to other drugs. Nevertheless, it is used very extensively, off label, in a wide variety of arrhythmias because of"},{"label":"A","text":"Prototypes, Mechanisms, and Effects its superior efficacy. Dofetilide and ibutilide are typical group 3 drugs. Sotalol is a Amiodarone causes microcrystalline deposits in the cornea chiral compound (ie, it has 2 optical isomers). One isomer is and skin, thyroid dysfunction (hyper- or hypothyroidism), par- an effective β blocker, and both isomers contribute to the antiar- esthesias, tremor, and pulmonary fibrosis. Amiodarone rarely rhythmic action. The clinical preparation contains both isomers. causes new arrhythmias, perhaps because it blocks calcium chan- Amiodarone is usually classified as a group 3 drug because it nels and β receptors as well as sodium and potassium channels. blocks the same K channels and markedly prolongs AP duration Dronedarone, an amiodarone analog that may be less toxic, is as well as blocking sodium channels. Dronedarone is a new drug, also approved. Like amiodarone, it acts on sodium, potassium, similar to amiodarone but less efficacious and less toxic. and calcium channels, but at present it is approved only for the The hallmark of group 3 drugs is prolongation of the AP dura- treatment of atrial fibrillation or flutter. tion. This AP prolongation is caused by blockade of IK potassium channels, chiefly IKr, that are responsible for the repolarization of GROUP 4 ANTIARRHYTHMICSthe AP (Figure 14–5). AP prolongation results in an increase in effective refractory period and reduces the ability of the heart to (CALCIUM L-TYPE CHANNEL BLOCKERS) respond to rapid tachycardias. Sotalol, ibutilide, dofetilide, and amiodarone (and group 1A drugs; see prior discussion) produce this A. Prototypes, Mechanisms, and Effects effect on most cardiac cells; the action of these drugs is, therefore, Verapamil is the prototype. Diltiazem is also an effective antiarrhyth- apparent in the ECG as an increase in QT interval (Table 14–1). mic drug. Nifedipine and the other dihydropyridines are not useful as antiarrhythmics, probably because they decrease arterial pressure"},{"label":"B","text":"Clinical Uses and Toxicities enough to evoke a compensatory sympathetic discharge to the heart. See the Drug Summary Table. The latter effect facilitates rather than suppresses arrhythmias. CHAPTER 14 Antiarrhythmic Drugs 137 Phase 2 (lCa and IK) Group 4 action 0 mV Phase 0 Note ICa −75 mV ERP Group 4 action Ca Na Outside Membrane Inside K1 Na Ca K Action potential currents Diastolic currents FIGURE 14–6 Schematic diagram of the effects of group 4 drugs in a calcium-dependent cardiac cell in the AV node (note that the AP upstroke in this figure is due mainly to calcium current). Group 4 drugs reduce inward calcium current during the AP and during phase 4 (wavy lines). As a result, conduction velocity is slowed in the AV node and refractoriness is prolonged. Pacemaker depolarization during phase 4 is slowed as well if caused by excessive calcium current. ERP, effective refractory period. Verapamil and diltiazem are effective in arrhythmias that must Adenosine is extremely effective in abolishing AV nodal arrhyth- traverse calcium-dependent cardiac tissue such as the AV node. mia, and because of its very low toxicity it has become the drug These agents cause a state- and use-dependent selective depres- of choice for this arrhythmia. Adenosine has an extremely short sion of calcium current (Figure 14–6). AV conduction velocity duration of action (about 15 s). Toxicity includes flushing and is decreased, and effective refractory period and PR interval are hypotension, but because of their short duration these effects do increased by these drugs (Table 14–1). not limit the use of the drug. Transient chest pain and dyspnea (probably due to bronchoconstriction) may also occur."},{"label":"B","text":"Clinical Use and Toxicities"},{"label":"B","text":"Potassium IonCalcium channel blockers are effective for converting AV nodal reentry (also known as nodal tachycardia) to normal sinus rhythm. Potassium depresses ectopic pacemakers, including those caused Their major use is in the prevention of these nodal arrhythmias by digitalis toxicity. Hypokalemia is associated with an increased in patients prone to recurrence. These drugs are orally active; incidence of arrhythmias, especially in patients receiving digitalis. and also available for parenteral use (see Drug Summary Table). Conversely, excessive potassium levels depress conduction and can The most important toxicity of these drugs is excessive depres- cause reentry arrhythmias. Therefore, when treating arrhythmias, sion of cardiac contractility, AV conduction, and blood pressure. serum potassium should be measured and normalized if abnormal. These agents should be avoided in ventricular tachycardias. See Chapter 12 for additional discussion of toxicity. Amiodarone has C. Magnesium Ion moderate calcium channel-blocking activity. Magnesium appears to have similar depressant effects as potassium on digitalis-induced arrhythmias. Magnesium also appears to be MISCELLANEOUS ANTIARRHYTHMIC effective in some cases of torsades de pointes arrhythmia. DRUGS NONPHARMACOLOGIC TREATMENT"},{"label":"A","text":"Adenosine OF ARRHYTHMIAS Adenosine is a normal component of the body, but when given in high doses (6–12 mg) as an intravenous bolus, the drug It should be noted that electrical methods of treatment of arrhythmias markedly slows or completely blocks conduction in the atrio- have become very important. These methods include (1) external ventricular node (Table 14–1), probably by hyperpolarizing this defibrillation, (2) implanted defibrillators, (3) implanted pacemakers, tissue (through increased IK1) and by reducing calcium current. and (4) radiofrequency ablation of arrhythmogenic foci via a catheter. 138 PART III Cardiovascular Drugs QUESTIONS 6. A 55-year-old man is admitted to the emergency department and is found to have an abnormal ECG. Overdose of an anti- Questions 1–2. A 76-year-old patient with rheumatoid arthritis arrhythmic drug is considered. Which of the following drugs procainamide. She is already receiving digoxin, hydrochlorothiazide, (A) Quinidine: Increased PR and decreased QT intervals and potassium supplements for her cardiac condition. (B) Flecainide: Increased PR, QRS, and QT intervals (C) Verapamil: Increased PR interval 1. In deciding on a treatment regimen with procainamide for (E) Metoprolol: Increased QRS duration (A) A possible drug interaction with digoxin suggests that digoxin blood levels should be obtained before and after 7. A 60-year-old man comes to the emergency department with starting procainamide severe chest pain. ECG reveals ventricular tachycardia with (B) Hyperkalemia should be avoided to reduce the likelihood occasional normal sinus beats, and ST-segment changes sug- of procainamide toxicity gestive of ischemia. A diagnosis of myocardial infarction is (C) Procainamide cannot be used if the patient has asthma made, and the man is admitted to the cardiac intensive care because it has a β-blocking effect unit. His arrhythmia should be treated immediately with (D) Procainamide has a duration of action of 36–40 h (A) Adenosine (E) Procainamide is not active by the oral route (B) Digoxin (C) Lidocaine 2. If this patient should take an overdose and manifest severe (D) Quinidine acute procainamide toxicity with markedly prolonged QRS, (A) A calcium chelator such as EDTA 8. Which of the following drugs slows conduction through the (B) Digitalis AV node and has its primary action directly on L-type calcium (D) Potassium chloride (A) Adenosine (E) Sodium lactate (B) Amiodarone (C) Diltiazem 3. A 57-year-old man is admitted to the emergency department (D) Esmolol with an irregular heart rate. The ECG shows an inferior (E) Flecainide myocardial infarction and ventricular tachycardia. Lidocaine (F) Lidocaine is ordered. When used as an antiarrhythmic drug, lidocaine (G) Mexiletine typically (H) Procainamide (A) Increases action potential duration (I) Quinidine (B) Increases contractility (C) Increases PR interval 9. When working in outlying fields, this 62-year-old farmer is (D) Reduces abnormal automaticity away from his house for 12–14 h at a time. He has an arrhyth- (E) Reduces resting potential mia that requires chronic therapy. Which of the following has the longest half-life of all antiarrhythmic drugs? 4. A 36-year-old woman with a history of poorly controlled thy- (A) Adenosine rotoxicosis has recurrent episodes of tachycardia with severe (B) Amiodarone shortness of breath. When she is admitted to the emergency (C) Disopyramide department with one of these episodes, which of the following (E) Flecainide (A) Amiodarone (F) Lidocaine (B) Disopyramide (G) Mexiletine (C) Esmolol (H) Procainamide (D) Quinidine (I) Quinidine (E) Verapamil (J) Verapamil"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["duplicate_option_labels","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","long_stem_manual_review","prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-a6ced8951a75","stem":"The drug effect shown in the diagram includes slowing of the upstroke of the AP and prolongation of repolarization or AP −80 mV duration. This is most typical of group 1A drugs. The answer is D, procainamide. Which of the following drugs does this agent most resemble? (A) Adenosine (B) Flecainide (C) Mexiletine SKILL KEEPER HARACTERISTICS (D) Procainamide OF a BLOCKERS (SEE CHAPTER 10) (E) Verapamil The major subgroups of β blockers and their pharmacologic ANSWERS features are conveniently listed in a table","type":"Image / Diagram Question","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-54fa785e7fdc","stem":"All the associations listed are incorrect except verapamil (see Combined β and Carvedilol, labetalol Table 14–1). This group 4 drug increases PR interval and has α blockade little effect on the other ECG variables. The answer is C. 140 PART III Cardiovascular Drugs C H E C K L I S T When you complete this chapter, you should be able to: ❑ Describe the distinguishing electrophysiologic and ECG effects of the 4 major groups of antiarrhythmic drugs and adenosine. ❑ List 2 or 3 of the most important drugs in each of the 4 groups. ❑ List the major toxicities of those drugs. ❑ Describe the mechanism of selective depression by local anesthetic antiarrhythmic agents. ❑ Explain how hyperkalemia, hypokalemia, or an antiarrhythmic drug can cause an arrhythmia. DRUG SUMMARY TABLE: Antiarrhythmic Drugs Mechanism Clinical Toxicities, Subclass of Action Applications Pharmacokinetics Interactions Group 1A Procainamide Use- and state-dependent block of Atrial and ventricular Oral and parenteral Increased arrhythmias, INa channels • some block of IK arrhythmias, especially • oral slow-release hypotension, lupus-like channels. Slowed conduction after myocardial forms available syndrome velocity and pacemaker activity infarction Duration: 2-3 h • prolonged action potential duration and refractory period Disopyramide: similar to procainamide but longer duration of action; toxicity includes antimuscarinic effects and heart failure Quinidine: similar to procainamide but toxicity includes cinchonism (tinnitus, headache, gastrointestinal disturbance) and thrombocytopenia Group 1B Lidocaine Highly selective use- and Ventricular arrhythmias IV and IM Central nervous system (CNS) state-dependent INa block; minimal post-myocardial Duration: 1-2 h sedation or excitation effect in normal tissue; no effect infarction and on IK digitalis-induced arrhythmias Mexiletine: similar to lidocaine but oral activity and longer duration of action Group 1C Flecainide Selective use- and state-dependent Refractory arrhythmias Oral Increased arrhythmias • CNS block of INa • slowed conduction Duration: 20 h excitation velocity and pacemaker activity Group 2 Propranolol Block of β receptors; slowed Postmyocardial infarction Oral, parenteral Bronchospasm • cardiac pacemaker activity as prophylaxis against Duration: 4-6 h depression, atrioventricular (AV) sudden death ventricular block, hypotension (see fibrillation; thyrotoxicosis Chapter 10) Metoprolol: similar to propranolol but β1-selective Esmolol: selective β1-receptor blockade; IV only, 10-min duration. Used in perioperative and thyrotoxicosis arrhythmias (Continued) CHAPTER 14 Antiarrhythmic Drugs 141 DRUG SUMMARY TABLE: Antiarrhythmic Drugs (Continued) Mechanism Clinical Toxicities, Subclass of Action Applications Pharmacokinetics Interactions Group 3 Amiodarone Strong IK block produces marked Refractory arrhythmias Oral, parenteral Thyroid abnormalities, deposits in prolongation of action potential • used off-label in many Half-life and duration skin and cornea, pulmonary and refractory period. Group 1 arrhythmias (broad of action: 1–10 wk fibrosis, optic neuritis • torsades is activity slows conduction velocity spectrum of therapeutic rare with amiodarone • groups 2 and 4 activity confer action) additional antiarrhythmic activity Sotalol IK block and β-adrenoceptor block Ventricular arrhythmias Oral Dose-related torsades de and atrial fibrillation Duration: 7 h pointes • cardiac depression Ibutilide Selective IK block • prolonged Treatment of acute atrial Ibutilide is IV only Torsades de pointes action potential and QT interval fibrillation Duration: 6 h Dofetilide Like ibutilide Treatment and Oral Torsades de pointes prophylaxis of atrial Duration: 7 h fibrillation Group 4 Verapamil State- and use-dependent AV nodal arrhythmias, Oral, parenteral Cardiac depression • constipation, ICa block slows conduction in AV especially in prophylaxis Duration: 7 h hypotension node and pacemaker activity • PR interval prolongation Diltiazem Like verapamil Rate control in atrial Oral, parenteral Like verapamil fibrillation Duration: 6 h Dihydropyridines: calcium channel blockers but not useful in arrhythmias; sometimes precipitate them Miscellaneous Adenosine Increase in diastolic IK of AV node Acute nodal tachycardias IV only Flushing, bronchospasm, that causes marked Duration: 10–15 s chest pain, headache hyperpolarization and conduction block • reduced ICa Potassium ion Increase in all K currents, Digitalis toxicity and Oral or IV Both hypokalemia and decreased automaticity, other arrhythmias if hyperkalemia are associated with decreased digitalis toxicity serum K is low arrhythmogenesis. Severe hyperkalemia causes cardiac arrest Magnesium Poorly understood, possible Digitalis arrhythmias IV Muscle weakness • severe ion increase in Na+/K+ ATPase activity and other arrhythmias if hypermagnesemia can cause serum Mg is low respiratory paralysis This page intentionally left blank C H A P T E R Diuretic Agents 15 Each segment of the nephron—proximal convoluted tubule based on these sites and processes in the nephron. Several other (PCT), thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle (TAL), drugs alter water excretion predominantly. The effects of the distal convoluted tubule (DCT), and cortical collecting tubule diuretic agents are predictable from knowledge of the function (CCT)—has a different mechanism for reabsorbing sodium and of the segment of the nephron in which they act. other ions. The subgroups of the sodium-excreting diuretics are Drugs used in renal disorders Drugs that modify Drugs that modify salt excretion water excretion PCT TAL DCT CCT Osmotic diuretics (mannitol) Loop K+-sparing ADH diuretics diuretics agonists (furosemide) (spironolactone) (desmopressin) Carbonic anhydrase ADH inhibitors Thiazides antagonists (acetazolamide) (hydrochlorothiazide) (conivaptan) RENAL TRANSPORT MECHANISMS PROXIMAL CONVOLUTED TUBULE (PCT) & DIURETIC DRUG GROUPS This segment carries out isosmotic reabsorption of amino acids, The kidney filters plasma water and solutes at the glomerulus at a glucose, and numerous cations. It is also the major site for sodium very high rate (180 L/day) and must recover a significant percent- chloride and sodium bicarbonate reabsorption. The proximal tubule age of most of these substances before excretion in the urine. The is responsible for 60–70% of the total reabsorption of sodium. major transport mechanisms for the recovery of ions and water in No currently available drug directly acts on NaCl reabsorp- the various segments of the nephron are shown in Figure 15–1. tion in the PCT. The mechanism for bicarbonate reabsorption Because the mechanisms for reabsorption of salt and water differ is shown in Figure 15–2. Bicarbonate itself is poorly reabsorbed in each of the 4 major tubular segments, the diuretics acting in through the luminal membrane, but conversion of bicarbonate these segments have differing mechanisms of action. Most diuretics to carbon dioxide via carbonic acid permits rapid reabsorption act from the luminal side of the membrane. An exception is the of the carbon dioxide. Bicarbonate can then be regenerated from aldosterone receptor antagonist group (eg, spironolactone and carbon dioxide within the tubular cell and transported into the eplerenone); these drugs enter the collecting tubule cell from the interstitium. Sodium is separately reabsorbed from the lumen in basolateral side and bind to the cytoplasmic aldosterone receptor. exchange for hydrogen ions (NHE3 transporter) and transported 143 144 PART III Cardiovascular Drugs High-Yield Terms to Learn Bicarbonate diuretic A diuretic that selectively increases sodium bicarbonate excretion. Example: a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor Diluting segment A segment of the nephron that removes solute without water; the thick ascending limb and the distal convoluted tubule are active salt-absorbing segments that are not permeable by water Hyperchloremic A shift in body electrolyte and pH balance involving elevated chloride, diminished bicarbonate metabolic acidosis concentration, and a decrease in pH in the blood. Typical result of bicarbonate diuresis Hypokalemic A shift in body electrolyte balance and pH involving a decrease in serum potassium and an metabolic alkalosis increase in blood pH. Typical result of loop and thiazide diuretic actions Nephrogenic Loss of urine-concentrating ability in the kidney caused by lack of responsiveness to antidiuretic diabetes insipidus hormone (ADH is normal or high) Pituitary diabetes Loss of urine-concentrating ability in the kidney caused by lack of antidiuretic hormone (ADH is insipidus low or absent) Potassium-sparing A diuretic that reduces the exchange of potassium for sodium in the collecting tubule; a drug that diuretic increases sodium and reduces potassium excretion. Example: aldosterone antagonists Uricosuric diuretic A diuretic that increases uric acid excretion, usually by inhibiting uric acid reabsorption in the proximal tubule. Example: ethacrynic acid Proximal NaHCO3 NaCl NaCl Ca2+ Distal convoluted (+PTH) convoluted tubule tubule 1 Proximal 4 straight tubule 7 K+ 7 K+ H+ 2 Ca2+ Collecting Glomerulus H2O Mg2+ 7 tubule Na+ Cortex ? 4 5 NaCl K+ 3 (+aldosterone) Outer medulla 2Cl− K+ Diuretics H