ely Typical examples of physiologic antagonists are the antagonism 150 mg. The therapeutic index is therefore approximately 150/3, of the bronchoconstrictor action of histamine by epinephrine’s or 50, in mice. Obviously, a full range of toxic doses cannot be bronchodilator action and glucagon’s antagonism of the cardiac ethically studied in humans. Furthermore, factors such as the depressant effects of propranolol. varying slopes of dose-response curves make this estimate a poor safety index even in animals. The therapeutic window, a more clinically useful index ofC. Chemical Antagonists safety, describes the dosage range between the minimum effec­ A chemical antagonist interacts directly with the drug being antago­ tive therapeutic concentration or dose, and the minimum toxic nized to remove it or to prevent it from binding to its target. A concentration or dose. For example, if the average minimum chemical antagonist does not depend on interaction with the agonist’s therapeutic plasma concentration of theophylline is 8 mg/L receptor (although such interaction may occur). Common examples and toxic effects are observed at 18 mg/L, the therapeutic of chemical antagonists are dimercaprol, a chelator of lead and some window is 8–18 mg/L. Both the therapeutic index and the other toxic metals, and pralidoxime, which combines avidly with the therapeutic window depend on the specific toxic effect used in phosphorus in organophosphate cholinesterase inhibitors. the determination. SKILL KEEPER: ALLOSTERIC ANTAGONISTS SIGNALING MECHANISMS (SEE CHAPTER 1) Once an agonist drug has bound to its receptor, some effector Describe the difference between a pharmacologic antagonist mechanism is activated. The receptor-effector system may be an and an allosteric inhibitor. How could you differentiate these enzyme in the intracellular space (eg, cyclooxygenase, a target of two experimentally? nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs) or in the membrane or extra­ cellular space (eg, acetylcholinesterase). Neurotransmitter reuptake CHAPTER 2 Pharmacodynamics 21 1 2 3 4 5 Steroid Tyrosine JAK-STAT Ion Channel GPCR kinase Drug Outside cell Membrane G Inside cell JAK A B Y Y~P X Y STAT FIGURE 2–6 Signaling mechanisms for drug effects. Five major cross-membrane signaling mechanisms are recognized: (1) transmembrane diffusion of the drug to bind to an intracellular receptor; (2) transmembrane enzyme receptors, whose outer domain provides the receptor function and inner domain provides the effector mechanism converting A to B; (3) transmembrane receptors that, after activation by an appro­ priate ligand, activate separate cytoplasmic tyrosine kinase molecules (JAKs), which phosphorylate STAT molecules that regulate transcription (Y, tyrosine; P, phosphate); (4) transmembrane channels that are gated open or closed by the binding of a drug to the receptor site; and (5) G protein-coupled receptors, which use a coupling protein to activate a separate effector molecule. (Modified and reproduced, with permission, from Katzung BG, editor: Basic & Clinical Pharmacology, 12th ed. McGraw-Hill, 2012: Fig. 2–5.) transporters (eg, the norepinephrine transporter, NET, and the mechanisms for receptor-effector systems have been defined dopamine transporter, DAT) are receptors for many drugs, eg, anti­ (Figure 2–6, Table 2–1). depressants and cocaine. Most antiarrhythmic drugs target voltage- activated ion channels in the membrane for sodium, potassium, or calcium. For the largest group of drug-receptor interactions, RECEPTOR REGULATION the drug is present in the extracellular space, whereas the effector mechanism resides inside the cell and modifies some intracellular Receptors are dynamically regulated in number, location, and process. These classic drug-receptor interactions involve signaling interaction with other molecules. Changes can occur over short across the membrane. Five major types of transmembrane-signaling times (minutes) and longer periods (days). TABLE 2–1 Types of transmembrane signaling receptors. Receptor Type Description Intracellular, often steroid Steroids, vitamin D, nitric oxide, and a few other highly membrane-permeant agents cross the membrane and receptor-like activate intracellular receptors. The effector molecule may be part of the receptor or separate Membrane-spanning receptor- Insulin, epidermal growth factor, and similar agents bind to the extracellular domain of molecules that incor­ effector enzymes porate tyrosine kinase enzyme activity in their intracellular domains. Most of these receptors dimerize upon activation Membrane receptors that bind Many cytokines activate receptor molecules that bind intracellular tyrosine kinase enzymes (Janus kinases, intracellular tyrosine kinase enzymes JAKs) that activate transcription regulators (signal transducers and activators of transcription, STATs) that (JAK-STAT receptors) migrate to the nucleus to bring about the final effect Ligand-activated or modulated Certain Na+/K+ channels are activated by drugs: acetylcholine activates nicotinic Na+/K+ channels, serotonin membrane ion channels activates 5-HT3 Na+/K+ channels. Benzodiazepines, barbiturates, and several other sedative hypnotics allosteri­ cally modulate GABA-activated Cl– channels"},{"label":"G","text":"protein-coupled receptors GPCRs consist of 7 transmembrane (7-TM) domains and when activated by extracellular ligands, bind trimeric (GPCRs) G proteins at the inner membrane surface and cause the release of activated Gα and Gβγ units. These activated units, in turn, modulate cytoplasmic effectors. The effectors commonly synthesize or release second messen­ gers such as cAMP, IP3, and DAG. GPCRs are the most common type of receptors in the body cAMP, cyclic adenosine monophosphate; IP3, inositol trisphosphate; DAG, diacylglycerol. 22 PART I Basic Principles Frequent or continuous exposure to agonists often results in short- 3. Prior to clinical trials in patients with heart failure, an animal term diminution of the receptor response, sometimes called tachy­ study was carried out to compare two new positive inotropic phylaxis. Several mechanisms are responsible for this phenomenon. drugs (A and B) to a current standard agent (C). The results of cardiac output measurements are shown in the graph below. First, intracellular molecules may block access of a G protein to the activated receptor molecule. For example, the molecule β-arrestin has been shown to bind to an intracellular loop of B the β adrenoceptor when the receptor is continuously activated. Beta-arrestin prevents access of the Gs-coupling protein and thus C outputdesensitizes the tissue to further β-agonist activation within min­ utes. Removal of the β agonist results in removal of β-arrestinand restoration of the full response after a few minutes or hours. cardiac A Second, agonist-bound receptors may be internalized by endo­ in cytosis, removing them from further exposure to extracellular molecules. The internalized receptor molecule may then be either Increase reinserted into the membrane (eg, morphine receptors) or degraded (eg, β adrenoceptors, epidermal growth factor receptors). In some cases, a cyclic internalization-reinsertion process may actually be necessary for normal functioning of the receptor-effector system. Log dose Third, continuous activation of the receptor-effector system Which of the following statements is correct?may lead to depletion of some essential substrate required for (A) Drug A is most effective downstream effects. For example, depletion of thiol cofactors may (B) Drug B is least potent be responsible for tolerance to nitroglycerin. In some cases, reple­ (C) Drug C is most potent tion of the missing substrate (eg, by administration of glutathione) (D) Drug B is more potent than drug C and more effective can reverse the tolerance. than drug A Long-term reductions in receptor number (downregulation) (E) Drug A is more potent than drug B and more effective than drug Cmay occur in response to continuous exposure to agonists. The opposite change (upregulation) occurs when receptor activation 4. A study was carried out in isolated intestinal smooth is blocked for prolonged periods (usually several days) by pharma­ muscle preparations to determine the action of a new drug cologic antagonists or by denervation. “novamine,” which in separate studies bound to the same receptors as acetylcholine. In the absence of other drugs, acetylcholine caused contraction of the muscle. Novamine QUESTIONS alone caused relaxation of the preparation. In the presence of a low concentration of novamine, the EC50 of acetylcholine"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["mcq_missing_options","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-425d7c8924cc","stem":"A 55-year-old woman with hypertension is to be treated with was unchanged, but the Emax was reduced. In the presence of a thiazide diuretic. Thiazide A in a dose of 5 mg produces a high concentration of novamine, extremely high concentra­ the same decrease in blood pressure as 500 mg of thiazide B. tions of acetylcholine had no effect. Which of the following Which of the following statements best describes these results? expressions best describes novamine? (A) Thiazide A is more efficacious than thiazide B (A) A chemical antagonist (B) Thiazide A is about 100 times more potent than thiazide B (B) An irreversible antagonist (C) Toxicity of thiazide A is less than that of thiazide B (C) A partial agonist (D) Thiazide A has a wider therapeutic window than thiazide B (D) A physiologic antagonist (E) Thiazide A has a longer half-life than thiazide B (E) A spare receptor agonist","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-50d377db1623","stem":"Graded and quantal dose-response curves are being used for 5. Beta adrenoceptors in the heart regulate cardiac rate and evaluation of a new antiasthmatic drug in the animal labora­ contractile strength. Several studies have indicated that in tory and in clinical trials. Which of the following statements humans and experimental animals, about 90% of β adreno­ best describes graded dose-response curves? ceptors in the heart are spare receptors. Which of the follow­ (A) More precisely quantitated than quantal dose-response ing statements about spare receptors is most correct? curves (A) Spare receptors, in the absence of drug, are sequestered (B) Obtainable from isolated tissue preparations but not in the cytoplasm from the study of intact subjects (B) Spare receptors may be detected by finding that the (C) Used to determine the maximal efficacy of the drug drug-receptor interaction lasts longer than the intracel­ (D) Used to determine the therapeutic index of the drug lular effect (E) Used to determine the variation in sensitivity of subjects (C) Spare receptors influence the maximal efficacy of the to the drug drug-receptor system (D) Spare receptors activate the effector machinery of the cell without the need for a drug (E) Spare receptors may be detected by the finding that the EC50 is smaller than the Kd for the agonist CHAPTER 2 Pharmacodynamics 23","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-1ab42f24ef6b","stem":"Two cholesterol-lowering drugs, X and Y, were studied in 9. Which of the curves in the graph describes the percentage a large group of patients, and the percentages of the group effect observed when a large dose of full agonist is present showing a specific therapeutic effect (35% reduction in low- throughout the experiment and the concentration of a partial density lipoprotein [LDL] cholesterol) were determined. The agonist is increased from low to very high levels? results are shown in the following table. (A) Curve 1 (B) Curve 2 Percent Responding Percent Responding (C) Curve 3 Drug Dose (mg) to Drug X to Drug Y (D) Curve 4 (E) Curve 5 5 1 10 10 5 20 10. Which of the curves in the graph describes the percentage of 20 10 50 binding of the partial agonist whose effect is shown by Curve 50 50 70 4 if the system has many spare receptors? (A) Curve 1 100 70 90 (B) Curve 2 200 90 100 (C) Curve 3 (D) Curve 4 Which of the following statements about these results is correct? (E) Curve 5 (A) Drug X is safer than drug Y (B) Drug Y is more effective than drug X (C) The 2 drugs act on the same receptors (D) Drug X is less potent than drug Y ANSWERS (E) The therapeutic index of drug Y is 10","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["possible_multiple_questions_in_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d76fdae2907e","stem":"year-old girl is brought to the clinic with severe asthmatic ing that the Vd of morphine in this patient is 200 L and wheezing. Because of the lack of other drugs, you decide to the half-life is 3 h, how much morphine did the patient inject use intravenous theophylline for treatment. The pharmaco­ 6 h earlier? kinetics of theophylline include the following average param­ (A) 25 mg eters: Vd 35 L; CL 48 mL/min; half-life 8 h. If an intravenous (B) 50 mg infusion of theophylline is started at a rate of 0.48 mg/min, (C) 100 mg how long would it take to reach 93.75% of the final steady- (D) 200 mg state concentration? (E) Not enough data to predict (A) Approximately 48 min (B) Approximately 7.4 h 10. Gentamicin, an aminoglycoside antibiotic, is sometimes given (C) Approximately 8 h in intermittent intravenous bolus doses of 100 mg 3 times (D) Approximately 24 h a day to achieve target peak plasma concentrations of about (E) Approximately 32 h 5 mg/L. Gentamicin’s clearance (normally 5.4 L/h/70 kg) is almost entirely by glomerular filtration. Your patient, however","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ec52d0fc3706","stem":"A 74-year-old retired mechanic is admitted with a myocardial is found to have a creatinine clearance one third of normal. infarction and a severe acute cardiac arrhythmia. You decide What should your modified dosage regimen for this patient be? to give lidocaine to correct the arrhythmia. A continuous (A) 20 mg 3 times a day intravenous infusion of lidocaine, 1.92 mg/min, is started at (B) 33 mg 3 times a day 8 am. The average pharmacokinetic parameters of lidocaine (C) 72 mg 3 times a day are: Vd 77 L; clearance 640 mL/min; half-life 1.4 h. What is (D) 100 mg 2 times a day the expected steady-state plasma concentration? (E) 150 mg 2 times a day (A) 40 mg/L (B) 3.0 mg/L (C) 0.025 mg/L (D) 7.2 mg/L (E) 3.46 mg/L CHAPTER 3 Pharmacokinetics 33 ANSWERS 6. The drug is being administered continuously and the steady- state concentration (Cpss) for a continuously administered","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Pharmacokinetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-3ee346756dae","stem":"693 × Vd/CL) and is equal to 11.5 L. This drug follows first-order kinetics, as indicated by the progressive approach to the steady-state plasma concentration. The answer is A. SKILL KEEPER 1 ELIMINATION (SEE CHAPTER 1) 5. The approach of the drug plasma concentration to steady-state concentration during continuous infusion follows a stereotypical curve (Figure 3–3) that rises rapidly at first and gradually reaches The 3 important drugs that follow zero-order rather than first- a plateau. It reaches 50% of steady state at 1 half-life, 75% at 2 order kinetics are ethanol, aspirin, and phenytoin. half-lives, 87.5% at 3, 93.75% at 4, and progressively halves the difference between its current level and 100% of steady state with each half-life. The answer is E, 32 h, or 4 half-lives. 34 PART I Basic Principles SKILL KEEPER 2 IRST-PASS EFFECT (SEE CHAPTER 1) The oral route of administration entails passage of the drug through the gastric and intestinal contents, the epithelium and other tissues of the intestinal wall, the portal blood, and the liver before it enters the systemic circulation for distribution to the body. Metabolism by enzymes in any of these tissues, expul­ sion by drug transporters, and excretion into the bile all may contribute to the first-pass effect of oral administration. C H E C K L I S T When you complete this chapter, you should be able to: ❑Estimate❑ the half-life of a drug based on its clearance and volume of distribution or from a graph of its plasma concentration over time. ❑Calculate❑ loading and maintenance dosage regimens for oral or intravenous admin­ istration of a drug when given the following information: minimum therapeutic con­ centration, minimum toxic concentration, oral bioavailability, clearance, and volume of distribution. ❑Calculate❑ the dosage adjustment required for a patient with impaired renal function. CHAPTER 3 Summary Table Major Concept Description Loading dose The dose required to achieve a specific plasma drug concentration level (Cp) with a single administration. Because this requires filling the volume of distribution (Vd), the calculation uses the volume of distribution (Vd) equation as: Loading dose = Cp(target) × Vd; has units of mg Maintenance dose The dose required for regular administration to maintain a target plasma level. Because this requires restoring the amount of drug lost to elimination (clearance, CL), the calculation uses the clearance equation as: Maintenance dose = Cp(target) × CL; has units of mg per time Half-life The half-life concept is useful in predicting the time course of falling drug levels after administration is stopped, and in predicting the time course of increase in drug level when repeated administration is begun—see Figure 3–3 Therapeutic window The therapeutic window is much more useful as a clinical measure of drug safety and as a guide to dosage than the older therapeutic index. The classic therapeutic index, TI, determined from animal measures of therapeutically effective dosage and lethal dosage, is inapplicable to human therapeutics, whereas the minimum therapeutic dos­ age and the minimum toxic dosage are readily determined in clinical trials Bioavailability The fraction or percentage of the dose of a drug that reaches the systemic circulation. The bioavailability of a drug given intravenously is therefore 100% C H A P T E R Drug Metabolism 4 All organisms are exposed to foreign chemical compounds terminate the action of many endogenous substances, evolution (xenobiotics) in the air, water, and food. To ensure elimi­ has provided metabolic pathways that alter such compounds’ nation of pharmacologically active xenobiotics as well as to activity and their susceptibility to excretion. Drug metabolism Phase I Phase II Genetic Induction Inhibition reactions reactions factors of drug of drug metabolism metabolism THE NEED FOR DRUG METABOLISM mechanisms for excreting undesirable substances produced within the body. Biotransformation of drugs is one such process. It is an Many cells in tissues that act as portals for entry of external molecules important mechanism by which the body terminates the action into the body (eg, pulmonary epithelium, intestinal epithelium) of many drugs. In some cases, it serves to activate prodrugs. Most contain transporter molecules (MDR family [P-glycoproteins], drugs are relatively lipid-soluble as given, a characteristic needed for MRP family, others) that expel unwanted molecules immediately absorption across membranes. The same property would result in after absorption. However, many foreign molecules evade these very slow removal from the body because the unchanged molecule gatekeepers and are absorbed. Therefore, all higher organisms, would also be readily reabsorbed from the urine in the renal tubule. especially terrestrial animals, require mechanisms for ridding them­ The body hastens excretion by transforming many drugs to less selves of toxic foreign molecules after they are absorbed, as well as lipid-soluble, less readily reabsorbed forms. High-Yield Terms to Learn Phase I reactions Reactions that convert the parent drug to a more polar (water-soluble) or more reactive product by unmasking or inserting a polar functional group such as ́OH, ́SH, or ́NH2 Phase II reactions Reactions that increase water solubility by conjugation of the drug molecule with a polar moiety such as glucuronate, acetate, or sulfate CYP isozymes Cytochrome P450 enzyme species (eg, CYP2D6 and CYP3A4) that are responsible for much of drug metabolism. Many isoforms of CYP have been recognized Enzyme induction Stimulation of drug-metabolizing capacity; usually manifested in the liver by increased synthesis of smooth endoplasmic reticulum (which contains high concentrations of phase I enzymes) P-glycoprotein, MDR-1 An ATP-dependent transport molecule found in many epithelial and cancer cells. The transporter expels drug molecules from the cytoplasm into the extracellular space. In epithelial cells, expulsion is via the external or luminal face 35 36 PART I Basic Principles TABLE 4–1 Examples of phase I drug-metabolizing reactions. Reaction Type Typical Drug Substrates Oxidations, P450 dependent Hydroxylation Amphetamines, barbiturates, phenytoin, warfarin N-dealkylation Caffeine, morphine, theophylline O-dealkylation Codeine N-oxidation Acetaminophen, nicotine S-oxidation Chlorpromazine, cimetidine, thioridazine Deamination Amphetamine, diazepam Oxidations, P450 independent Amine oxidation Epinephrine Dehydrogenation Chloral hydrate, ethanol Reductions Chloramphenicol, clonazepam, dantrolene, naloxone Hydrolyses Esters Aspirin, clofibrate, procaine, succinylcholine Amides Indomethacin, lidocaine, procainamide TYPES OF METABOLIC REACTIONS B. Phase II Reactions Phase II reactions are synthetic reactions that involve addition","type":"Calculation","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phase I Reactions (conjugation) of subgroups to —OH, —NH2, and —SH functions Phase I reactions include oxidation (especially by the cytochrome on the drug molecule. The subgroups that are added include gluc­ P450 group of enzymes, also called mixed-function oxidases), uronate, acetate, glutathione, glycine, sulfate, and methyl groups. reduction, deamination, and hydrolysis. Examples of phase I drug Most of these groups are relatively polar and make the product less substrates are listed in Table 4–1. These enzymes are found in high lipid-soluble than the original drug molecule. Examples of phase II concentrations in the smooth endoplasmic reticulum of the liver. reactions are listed in Table 4–2. Like phase I enzymes, phase II They are not highly selective in their substrates, so a relatively enzymes are not very selective. Drugs that are metabolized by both small number of P450 isoforms are able to metabolize thousands routes may undergo phase II metabolism before or after phase I. of drugs. Of the drugs metabolized by phase I cytochrome P450s, approximately 75% are metabolized by just two: CYP3A4/5 or SITES OF DRUG METABOLISM CYP2D6. CYP3A4 and CYP3A5 alone are responsible for the metabolism of approximately 50% of drugs. Nevertheless, some The most important organ for drug metabolism is the liver. The selectivity can be detected, and optical enantiomers, in particular, kidneys play an important role in the metabolism of some drugs. are often metabolized at different rates. A few dr