kely to increase the dura­ (G) Succinylcholine tion of action of a drug that is metabolized by CYP3A4 in the (H) Verapamil liver? 9. Which of the following drugs is an established inhibitor of (A) Chronic administration of rifampin during therapy with P-glycoprotein (P-gp) drug transporters? the drug in question (A) Cimetidine (B) Chronic therapy with amiodarone (B) Ethanol (C) Displacement from tissue-binding sites by another drug (C) Ketoconazole (D) Increased cardiac output (D) Procainamide (E) Chronic administration of carbamazepine (E) Quinidine","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["possible_multiple_questions_in_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fac361a99beb","stem":"Reports of cardiac arrhythmias caused by unusually high (F) Ritonavir blood levels of 2 antihistamines, terfenadine and astemizole, (G) Succinylcholine led to their removal from the market. Which of the following (H) Verapamil best explains these effects? 10. Which of the following cytochrome isoforms is responsible (A) Concomitant treatment with rifampin for metabolizing the largest number of drugs? (B) Use of these drugs by chronic alcoholics (A) CYP1A2 (C) Use of these drugs by chronic smokers (B) CYP2C9 (D) Treatment of these patients with ketoconazole, an azole (C) CYP2C19 antifungal agent (D) CYP2D6","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antifungal","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["possible_multiple_questions_in_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-cb969c97fa3b","stem":"Which of the following agents, when used in combination (E) CYP3A4 with other anti-HIV drugs, permits dose reductions?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5138be82ecef","stem":"Amiodarone is an important antiarrhythmic drug and has a well-documented ability to inhibit the hepatic metabolism of many drugs. The answer is A. C H E C K L I S T When you complete this chapter, you should be able to: ❑List❑ the major phase I and phase II metabolic reactions. Know which P450 isoform is responsible for the greatest number of important reactions. ❑Describe❑ the mechanism of hepatic enzyme induction and list 3 drugs that are known to cause it. ❑List❑ 3 drugs that inhibit the metabolism of other drugs. ❑Describe❑ some of the effects of smoking, liver disease, and kidney disease on drug elimination. ❑Describe❑ the pathways by which acetaminophen is metabolized (1) to harmless prod­ ucts if normal doses are taken and (2) to hepatotoxic products if an overdose is taken. CHAPTER 4 Summary Table Major Concept Description Drug metabolism vs drug Termination of drug action requires either removal of the drug from the body (excretion) or modification of elimination the drug molecule (metabolism) so that it no longer has an effect. Both methods constitute drug elimination, and both are very important in the clinical use of drugs. Almost all drugs (or their metabolites) are eventu­ ally excreted, but for many, excretion occurs only some time after they have been metabolized to inactive products Induction and inhibition of drug A large number of drugs alter their own metabolism and the metabolism of other drugs either by inducing the metabolism synthesis of larger amounts of the metabolizing enzymes (usually P450 enzymes in the liver) or by inhibiting those enzymes. Some drugs both inhibit (acutely) and induce (with chronic administration) drug metabolism Pharmacogenomic variation in Genetic variations in drug metabolism undoubtedly occur for many drugs. Specific differences have been drug metabolism defined for (1) succinylcholine and similar esters, (2) procainamide and similar amines, and (3) a miscellaneous group that includes β blockers, antidepressants, and others (see Chapter 5) Toxic metabolism Some substances are metabolized to toxic molecules by drug-metabolizing enzymes. Important examples include methyl alcohol, ethylene glycol, and, at high doses or in the presence of liver disease, acetaminophen. See Figure 4–1 and Chapter 23 C H A P T E R Pharmacogenomics 5 Pharmacogenomics is a rapidly growing area of knowledge drug transport. Application of genomic analysis of individual regarding the genetic variations that influence drug metabo­ patients to selection of specific drugs and drug dosage is under lism and drug effects. Most of the research in this field to investigation. date has involved phase I or phase II drug metabolism and Pharmacogenomics Definitions Enzymes Transporters Immune system INTRODUCTION genes for some phase I and phase II enzymes and some drug trans­ porters. Examples of these genetic determinants of drug metabo­ The inheritance of genetic information via the double DNA helix lism and transport are the subject of this chapter. is now well-understood. The decoding of the human genome and of many animal and plant genomes has opened a field of research PHASE I ENZYMESinto the molecular basis of variations between individuals and among populations. The identification of the specific genes (or CYP2D6, CYP2C19, CYP3A4/5, and dihydropyrimidine dehy­groups of genes) that affect drug responses is still incomplete, but drogenase are among the drug-metabolizing enzymes most care­knowledge about a small number of these genes of pharmacologic fully studied (Table 5–1).significance has suggested the possibility that “personalized medi­ cine” is possible and may become practical in the near future. A. CYP2D6 Personalized medicine denotes clinical treatment that takes This enzyme is responsible for the hepatic metabolism of 20%into account the genetic factors that contribute to disease and of commonly used drugs. More than 100 polymorphisms of thethe pharmacogenomic factors that influence the response to drug CYP2D6 gene have been discovered, but only 9 are common.treatment in specific individuals. Intense academic and com­ CYP2D6 polymorphisms are especially important in patients receiv­mercial research is currently directed at discovering these factors. ing codeine because this enzyme converts codeine to its active metabo­Research is also directed at developing accurate and inexpensive lite, morphine. Several deaths due to respiratory depression have beentests for pharmacogenetic factors in individual patients. reported in children who were believed to be ultrarapid metabolizers. As noted in Chapter 4, important genetic variations in drug metabolism exist between individuals. Furthermore, genetic dis­","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Drugs","subtopic":"Antiarrhythmic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"B","text":"CYP2C19eases alter many functions that are drug targets. The identifica­ tion of specific genes that control the expression of the molecules CYP2C19 is responsible for the hepatic metabolism of a involved and the variants (polymorphisms) of those genes has small number of very important drugs (clopidogrel, pro­ become the subject of intense research over the last 10–20 years. pranolol, omeprazole, diazepam, and tricyclic antidepressants). At present, much data are available regarding the variants of the Because reduced metabolism of clopidogrel results in lower 41 42 PART I Basic Principles High-Yield Terms to Learn Pharmacogenetics Synonym for pharmacogenomics; the study of genetic factors that affect drug responses Single nucleotide A single base pair substitution in the genome that occurs in >1% of a subject population (cf polymorphism (SNP) mutation) Mutation A polymorphism that occurs in the genome of <1% of a population; more generally, any change in the genetic material Allele One of 2 or more alternative forms of a gene. Almost all genes are represented by 2 alleles in the genome (because 22 of the 23 human chromosomes are paired). Allele variants are denoted “*3,” “*5,” etc Diplotype Representation of the alleles for a specific gene on both chromosomes of a pair. Thus, the gene for the enzyme CYP2D6 with allele *3 on one chromosome and *5 on the other would be denoted CYP2D6*3/*5 Haplotype A series of alleles found in a linked locus on a chromosome Genotype, phenotype Characteristics of the DNA (genotype) and the physiology and biochemistry (phenotype) expressed by the DNA of an individual or population Indels Insertions or deletions of one or more nucleotide bases in genes Synonymous SNP A single nucleotide variation (SNP) that codes for the same amino acid when read out; no change of function (phenotype) results Nonsynonymous An SNP that results in substitution of a different amino acid when read out; a change in function (missense) SNP may result Copy number variation Variation in the number of copies of a gene. An increased number of copies commonly results in a (CNV) gain of function phenotype and vice versa PM, IM, EM, UM Poor metabolizer, intermediate metabolizer, extensive metabolizer, and ultrarapid metabolizer, respectively. These terms describe individuals with varying rates of metabolism of a specific drug or the genomes responsible in such individuals mtDNA, Y-DNA mtDNA is the DNA found in mitochondria; it is normally inherited only through the maternal line. Y-DNA is the DNA found in the Y chromosome and is therefore inherited through the paternal line Genome-Wide Association Analysis of the complete genomes of a population of individuals with regard to the frequency of Study (GWAS) association of specific allelic variations with a specific phenotype concentrations of its active metabolite, reduced function poly­ polymorphisms in the DYPD gene result in increased toxicity and morphisms in this enzyme reduce the efficacy of clopidogrel require reduced dosage. and increase the risk of clotting in patients with coronary artery disease. Conversely, gain of function results in increased risk of E. Multiple Enzyme Polymorphisms: CYP2C9 and VCORC1 bleeding. Poor metabolizers and IMs should receive alternative CYP2C9 and vitamin K epoxide reductase complex subunit 1 drugs prasugrel or ticagrelor, not clopidogrel. (VCORC1) are responsible for the inactivation of S-warfarin. Some mutations of the VCORC1 gene lead to spontaneous bleed­"},{"label":"C","text":"CYP3A4 and CYP3A5 ing disorders. Reduced function polymorphisms in both genes result in increased warfarin action and enhanced risk of bleeding.CYP3A4/5 are responsible for the metabolism of over 50% of Algorithms have been developed to predict the optimal dosage ofdrugs in common use. Some polymorphisms with important warfarin, but clinical trials of these algorithms have not shownethnic variability have been described, but relatively few appear to improved anticoagulant control thus far.alter pharmacokinetics to a clinically significant degree."},{"label":"D","text":"Dihydropyrimidine Dehydrogenase (DPD) PHASE II ENZYMES DPD is responsible for the clearance of 5-fluorouracil (5-FU), a first-line prodrug agent for the treatment of colorectal cancer. A. Uridine 5-diphospho-(UDP) glucuronosyltransferase Capecitabine and tegafur are oral prodrugs converted in the body (UGT1A1) to 5-FU. In the body, 5-FU is converted to cytotoxic 5-fluorouri­ UGT1A1 is involved in the hepatic excretion of small molecules dine 5′-monophosphate (5-FUMP) and 5-fluoro-2′-deoxyuridine- into the bile. UGT1A1 contributes to the clearance of SN-38, the 5′-monophosphate (5-FdUMP) (see Chapter 54). Nonfunctional bioactive metabolite of irinotecan, a cytotoxic agent used in the CHAPTER 5 Pharmacogenomics 43 TABLE 5–1 Polymorphisms associated with altered drug responses. Functional Element Alleles or SNPs of Major Importance Examples of Drugs Affected Phase I enzyme CYP2C9 *2, *3: decreased function Warfarin, phenytoin, antidiabetic sulfonylurea metabolism slowed, toxicity increased CYP2C19 *17: increased function, Increased or decreased clopidogrel active metabolite *2, *3: decreased function CYP2D6 *1, *2: increased function Codeine converted to morphine. Increased function associated with increased toxicity; decreased function associated with *3, *4, *5: decreased function decreased analgesia. Increased toxicity of many other drugs CYP3A4, *1, *8, *11, *13, *16, *17: decreased function Metabolism of some dihydropyridines, cyclosporine, tacrolimus reduced; increased toxicity 3A5 (SNPs more common in 3A5) *3, *5, *6, *7: decreased function Dihydropyrimidine DPYD *2A, *13, rs67376798: reduced function Increased toxicity from pyrimidine cancer chemotherapeutic dehydrogenase (DPD) agents, eg, 5-FU Phase II enzyme UGT1A1 UGT1A1*28 Increased irinotecan toxicity TPMT *2, *3 Increased thiopurine (azathioprine, 6-mercaptopurine,"}],"priority":2,"status":"quarantine","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAn